      "text": "8935 + 53 = 8988.\n\nWait, that doesn't seem right. Maybe I made a mistake in breaking down the numbers or in the calculations. Let me check again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using another method would be better. Let's try the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\nMultiply 29 by 69.\n\nFirst, write them down:\n\n\u2003\u200329\n\n\u00d7 69\n\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 29 by 9 (the units digit of 69):\n\n29 \u00d7 9 = 261\n\nThen multiply 29 by 60 (since the 6 is in the tens place, so 6\u00d710):\n\n29 \u00d7 60 = 1740\n\nNow add those two results together:\n\n261 + 1740 = 2001\n\nWait, that seems straightforward. But earlier when I broke it down using distributive property, I got a different answer. Hmm, so where did I go wrong?\n\nLet me recalculate the first method. The user asked for 29 * 69, but in my initial thought process, I started with 75 and 70, then switched to 93 and 87, then 46 and 35. Wait a second, that's all over the place. Maybe I got confused with different numbers. The problem was actually 29 and 69. Let me focus on that.\n\nLet me try the distributive property again properly with the correct numbers. Let's break down 29 and 69 into more manageable parts.\n\nLet me consider 29 as 30 - 1, and 69 as 70 - 1. Then:\n\n(30 - 1)(70 - 1) = 30*70 - 30*1 - 1*70 + 1*1\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n30\u00d770 = 2100\n\n30\u00d71 = 30\n\n1\u00d770 = 70\n\n1\u00d71 = 1\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\n2100 - 30 -70 +1 = 2100 - 100 +1 = 2001. Okay, that matches the standard algorithm result. So the correct answer should be 2001.\n\nWait, but in my first approach with the incorrect breakdown, I must have messed up the numbers. Probably because I was distracted by different numbers initially. So the correct product is 2001. Let me just confirm with another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, using the standard vertical multiplication:\n\n\u200329\n\n\u00d769\n\n--------\n\nFirst multiply 29 by 9: 29\u00d79 = 261\n\nThen multiply 29 by 60: 29\u00d760 = 1740\n\nAdd them: 261 + 1740 = 2001. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using another distributive approach:\n\n29 \u00d769 = (20 +9)(60 +9) = 20\u00d760 +20\u00d79 +9\u00d760 +9\u00d79\n\nCalculates as:\n\n20\u00d760 = 1200\n\n20\u00d79=180\n\n9\u00d760=540\n\n9\u00d79=81\n\nAdd them all: 1200 +180 = 1380; 1380 +540=1920; 1920 +81=2001. Same answer again.\n\nSo the initial confusion was because I started thinking of different numbers, but after correcting and applying the proper methods, the answer is 2001. I need to make sure I follow through with the correct numbers and methods without getting distracted by other numbers. So the product of 29 and 69 is indeed 2001.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 29 and 69 is \\boxed{2001}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 29 and 69, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into more manageable parts:\n\n1. Break down 29 as \\(30 - 1\\) and 69 as \\(70 - 1\\):\n   \\[\n   (30 - 1)(70 - 1)\n   \\]\n2. Apply the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   30 \\times 70 - 30 \\times 1 - 1 \\times 70 + 1 \\times 1\n   \\]\n3. Calculate each term:\n   \\[\n   30 \\times 70 = 2100\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   30 \\times 1 = 30\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1 \\times 70 = 70\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1 \\times 1 = 1\n   \\]\n4. Combine the results:\n   \\[\n   2100 - 30 - 70 + 1 = 2100 - 100 + 1 = 2001\n   \\]\n\nWe can also verify this using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n1. Multiply 29 by 9:\n   \\[\n   29 \\times 9 = 261\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 29 by 60:\n   \\[\n   29 \\times 60 = 1740\n   \\]\n3. Add the results:\n   \\[\n   261 + 1740 = 2001\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 29 and 69 is \\(\\boxed{2001}\\).",
