      "text": "269.\n\nWait a second, that can't be right. Let me verify with another method. Maybe using the distributive property. 54 times 61 can be broken down into (50 + 4) \u00d7 (60 + 1). Then expanding that: 50\u00d760 + 50\u00d71 + 4\u00d760 + 4\u00d71. Calculating each term: 3000 + 50 + 240 + 4. Adding those together: 3000 + 50 is 3050, plus 240 is 3290, plus 4 is 3294. So that's different from my previous answer of 269. Hmm, so I must have made a mistake in my initial calculation. Let me go back and check where I went wrong.\n\nWait, hold on. The original problem was 43 multiplied by 23. Wait, 43 times 23. Wait, in my thinking above, I was talking about 54 times 61? That was a mistake. Maybe I got confused with another problem. Let me start over.\n\nThe question is the product of 43 and 23. Let me approach this step by step. Let me write it down as:\n\nFirst, 43 \u00d7 23. To compute this, I can use the standard multiplication method. Let me write it vertically:\n\n\u2003\u00a043\n\u00d7 23\n------\n\nStarting with the units digit of the bottom number (which is 3) multiplied by 43:\n\n3 \u00d7 43 = 129. I'll write that down.\n\nThen, moving to the tens digit of the bottom number (which is 2), since it's in the tens place, it's actually 20. So, 20 \u00d7 43. Let me compute that. 2 \u00d7 43 = 86, and since it's 20, we add a zero at the end, making it 860. Wait, no, actually, when writing it out vertically, you would shift it one position to the left because it's the tens place. So:\n\n\u2003\u00a043\n\u00d7 23\n------\n\u00a0129  (this is 3 \u00d7 43)\n+860  (this is 20 \u00d7 43, written as 86 shifted one left)\n------\n\u00a0989\n\nAdding 129 and 860 together: 129 + 860. Let's see: 129 + 800 is 929, then +60 is 989. So that gives me a total of 989.\n\nWait, but let me verify using another method to be sure. Let's use the distributive property again. Breaking down 43 and 23 into tens and units:\n\n43 is 40 + 3, and 23 is 20 + 3. So multiplying them using FOIL (First, Outer, Inner, Last):\n\n(40 + 3)(20 + 3) = 40\u00d720 + 40\u00d73 + 3\u00d720 + 3\u00d73.\n\nCalculating each part:\n\n40\u00d720 = 800,\n\n40\u00d73 = 120,\n\n3\u00d720 = 60,\n\n3\u00d73 = 9.\n\nAdding all those together: 800 + 120 = 920, plus 60 is 980, plus 9 is 989. So that matches the previous result. Okay, so that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, I could add 43 twenty-three times. Let's see, that would be tedious, but perhaps adding in chunks. For example, 43 \u00d7 20 is 860, and 43 \u00d7 3 is 129, so adding those together gives 860 + 129 = 989. Same answer.\n\nSo, after two different methods, I get 989. Therefore, the product of 43 and 23 is 989.\n\nWait, but earlier I had a muddled thought where I confused numbers like 54 and 61, but that was probably a brain fart. I need to make sure I stay focused here. Let me just confirm with a calculator approach, perhaps using another breakdown.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the standard algorithm again:\n\n43 \u00d7 23. Let me write it again:\n\n\u2003 43\n\u00d7 23\n-------\nFirst, 3 \u00d7 43: 3\u00d740=120, 3\u00d73=9, so 129.\n\nThen 20 \u00d7 43: 20\u00d740=800, 20\u00d73=60, so 860. Adding 129 + 860 = 989. Yep.\n\nSo, I think that's correct. So the answer should be 989. I don't see any errors in the calculations now. Initially, I got confused with another problem but then corrected myself. So I'm confident the answer is 989.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 43 and 23 is \\boxed{989}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 43 and 23, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 43 \\\\\n\\times 23 \\\\\n\\hline\n   129 \\quad (\\text{3 times 43}) \\\\\n+860 \\quad (\\text{20 times 43, shifted one position to the left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   989 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBreaking it down using the distributive property:\n- \\(43 \\times 23 = (40 + 3)(20 + 3)\\)\n- Expanding the terms: \\(40 \\times 20 + 40 \\times 3 + 3 \\times 20 + 3 \\times 3\\)\n- Calculating each term:\n  - \\(40 \\times 20 = 800\\)\n  - \\(40 \\times 3 = 120\\)\n  - \\(3 \\times 20 = 60\\)\n  - \\(3 \\times 3 = 9\\)\n- Adding the results: \\(800 + 120 + 60 + 9 = 989\\)\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 43 and 23 is \\(\\boxed{989}\\).",
