s point to 1008. \n\nAnother check: 84\u00d712. Let me use the standard multiplication vertically:\n\n\u2003\u00a084\n\n\u00d7\u00a012\n\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 84 by 2:\n\n8\u00d72 = 16, write down 6, carryover 1.\n\n4\u00d72 = 8, plus the carried over 1 is 9. So first partial product is 168.\n\nThen multiply 84 by 1 (but since it's the tens place, it's actually 10):\n\n8\u00d71 = 8, but shifted one position to the left, so it's in the tens place.\n\n4\u00d71 = 4, also shifted left. So the second partial product is 840.\n\nAdding 168 and 840 gives 1008. \n\nAll methods confirm the result is 1008. I think that's solid. Initially, I was confused because I started with a different problem, but after correcting myself, everything checks out. So the answer should be 1008.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 84 and 12 is \\boxed{1008}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 84 and 12, we can use the distributive property and the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nFirst, using the distributive property:\n\\[\n84 \\times 12 = 84 \\times (10 + 2) = (84 \\times 10) + (84 \\times 2)\n\\]\nCalculating each term separately:\n\\[\n84 \\times 10 = 840\n\\]\n\\[\n84 \\times 2 = 168\n\\]\nAdding the results together:\n\\[\n840 + 168 = 1008\n\\]\n\nUsing the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   84 \\\\\n\\times 12 \\\\\n\\hline\n   168 \\quad (\\text{84 multiplied by 2}) \\\\\n+840 \\quad (\\text{84 multiplied by 10, shifted one position to the left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n  1008 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 84 and 12 is \\(\\boxed{1008}\\).",
