t me check using another method to verify.\n\nAlternatively, using the distributive property:\n\n26 \u00d7 76 = (20 + 6) \u00d7 (70 + 6)\n\nMultiply each term:\n\n20\u00d770 = 1400\n\n20\u00d76 = 120\n\n6\u00d770 = 420\n\n6\u00d76 = 36\n\nAdding them up: 1400 + 120 = 1520; 1520 + 420 = 1940; 1940 + 36 = 1976. That matches.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do 26\u00d770 plus 26\u00d76:\n\n26\u00d770 = 1820\n\n26\u00d76 = 156\n\nAdding gives 1820 + 156 = 1976. Correct.\n\nHmm, so in my initial thinking I must have confused the numbers with different problems, leading to confusion, but now I corrected myself. The correct answer is 1976.\n\nWait, but let me check with a calculator method.\n\n26 \u00d7 76:\n\n26 \u00d7 70 = 1820\n\n26 \u00d7 6 = 156\n\nAdding together: 1820 + 156 = 1976. Yep, that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, 76 \u00d7 26:\n\n76 \u00d7 20 = 1520\n\n76 \u00d7 6 = 456\n\n1520 + 456 = 1976. Same result.\n\nTherefore, the product of 26 and 76 is indeed 1976.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 26 and 76 is \\boxed{1976}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 26 and 76, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   26 \\\\\n\\times 76 \\\\\n\\hline\n  156 \\quad \\text{(26 \u00d7 6)} \\\\\n1820 \\quad \\text{(26 \u00d7 70, shifted one position to the left)} \\\\\n\\hline\n 1976 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBreaking it down using the distributive property:\n\\[\n26 \\times 76 = (20 + 6) \\times (70 + 6)\n\\]\n\\[\n= 20 \\times 70 + 20 \\times 6 + 6 \\times 70 + 6 \\times 6\n\\]\n\\[\n= 1400 + 120 + 420 + 36\n\\]\n\\[\n= 1400 + 120 = 1520\n\\]\n\\[\n1520 + 420 = 1940\n\\]\n\\[\n1940 + 36 = 1976\n\\]\n\nAnother verification method:\n\\[\n26 \\times 70 = 1820\n\\]\n\\[\n26 \\times 6 = 156\n\\]\n\\[\n1820 + 156 = 1976\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 26 and 76 is \\boxed{1976}.",
