 is close to 15, and 31 is close to 30. 15\u00d730=450. Since both numbers are a bit higher, the actual product should be a bit more than 450. 496 is reasonable. Alternatively, 16\u00d730=480, so adding another 16 gives 496. That seems right.\n\nI think that's solid. So the answer is 496.\n\n**:**\n\nThe product of 16 and 31 can be calculated using the standard multiplication method:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 16 \\\\\n\\times 31 \\\\\n\\hline\n   \\quad 16 \\quad (\\text{16 multiplied by 1}) \\\\\n+ 480 \\quad \\, (\\text{16 multiplied by 30, shifted one position to the left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   496 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBreaking it down:\n1. **16 \u00d7 1** = 16  \n2. **16 \u00d7 30** = 480  \n3. **16 + 480** = 496  \n\nThus, the product of 16 and 31 is \\boxed{496}.",
