The digits of 85 go on top, and 58 on the right.\n\nSo:\n\n8 | 5\n\n5\n\n8\n\nWait, maybe I need to draw it mentally. Each digit pair multiplies, then you add diagonally.\n\nFirst, 8\u00d75=40, so write 4 and 0 in the top left cell.\n\n8\u00d78=64, so top right cell has 6 and 4.\n\nThen 5\u00d75=25, bottom left cell has 2 and 5.\n\n5\u00d78=40, bottom right cell has 4 and 0.\n\nNow, add diagonally from the right:\n\nThe rightmost diagonal has 0 (from top right) and 0 (from bottom right), so 0.\n\nNext diagonal: 4 (from top right) + 4 (bottom right's first digit?) Wait, maybe I'm mixing up. Let me think again.\n\nActually, in lattice, each cell's digits are split by a diagonal line, the top left and bottom right. So, the diagonals for adding are from the bottom left to top right.\n\nLet me try again:\n\nThe grid cells after multiplication would be:\n\nFirst cell (top left corner): 8\u00d75=40 \u2192 split into 4 (top) and 0 (bottom).\n\nSecond cell (top right): 8\u00d78=64 \u2192 6 (top) and 4 (bottom).\n\nThird cell (bottom left): 5\u00d75=25 \u2192 2 (top) and 5 (bottom).\n\nFourth cell (bottom right):5\u00d78=40 \u2192 4 (top) and 0 (bottom).\n\nNow, the diagonals:\n\nFirst diagonal (rightmost): the bottom of the top right cell (4) and the bottom of the bottom right cell (0). Adding those: 4+0=4. Write that down.\n\nSecond diagonal: the top of the bottom right cell (4), the bottom of the top left cell (0), and the bottom of the bottom left cell (5). Wait, maybe I'm confused.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the diagonals go from the top right to bottom left. Let me see:\n\nThe diagonals are vertical lines from top to bottom. The rightmost diagonal is just the bottom part of the top right cell (4) and the bottom part of the bottom right cell (0). So 4 +0 =4.\n\nNext diagonal to the left: the top part of the bottom right cell (4), the bottom part of the top left cell (0), and the bottom part of the bottom left cell (5). Wait, maybe not. Let me think of the standard lattice