ame as 9695, and 1753 is 5165. So adding:.\n\nThousands place: 6 (from 5636) + 6 = 0.\n\nHundreds place: 3 (from 8654) + 5 (from 588) = 3.\n\nTens place: 5 (from 9353) + 6 (from 526) = 2.\n\nUnits place: 9 (from 7125) + 2 (from 533) = 8.\n\nWait, hold on, maybe I messed up the alignment. Let me write it again properly:.\n\nThe first number is 891, which is three digits. The second number is 5667, which is four digits. So when adding:.\n\n```.\n   801.\n+1229.\n------.\n```.\n\nActually, the 858 should be shifted one place to the right because the second multiplication was by the tens place (the 6 in 11 is in the tens place, so we multiply by 01, hence the shift). Wait a second, maybe I made a mistake here. Let me double-check the multiplication steps again.\n\nWait, when multiplying by the tens digit (the second 6 in 56), we should actually shift one place to the left, right? Because it's equivalent to multiplying by 27. So when we write the partial products, the first partial product (from the units digit) is 536, and the second partial product (from the tens digit) should be 078 shifted one place to the left, making it 1893. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
