rtical multiplication:\n\n   65\nx 21\n--------\nFirst line: 65 x 1 = 65\n\nSecond line: 65 x 20 = 1300 (since 2 is in the tens place, so it's 20)\n\nAdd them:\n\n  1300\n\n+  65\n\n--------\n\n1365\n\nYes, that adds up correctly. So I believe the correct answer is 1365. I don't see any mistakes in these calculations. So I think that's it.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 65 and 21 is \\boxed{1365}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 65 and 21, we can use the distributive property and break down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n\\[\n65 \\times 21 = 65 \\times (20 + 1)\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(65 \\times 20\\):\n\n\\[\n65 \\times 20 = 1300\n\\]\n\nThen, calculate \\(65 \\times 1\\):\n\n\\[\n65 \\times 1 = 65\n\\]\n\nAdd the results of these two calculations together:\n\n\\[\n1300 + 65 = 1365\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify this using the standard multiplication method:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   65 \\\\\n\\times 21 \\\\\n\\hline\n   65 \\quad (\\text{65 times 1}) \\\\\n+1300 \\quad (\\text{65 times 20, shifted one position to the left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n  1365 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 65 and 21 is \\(\\boxed{1365}\\).",
