. Wait, this is getting confusing. Maybe I misread the question initially?\n\nWait the user's question is: \"What is the Product of 72 and 63?\" So yeah, that's 72\u00d763. But in my first line, I said \"product of 38 and 85\". That must be a mistake. Oh no, maybe I was thinking of a different problem earlier. That's confusing. Let me start over.\n\nSo the correct question is 72 multiplied by 63. Let me do it step by step properly.\n\nFirst, write 72 on top and 63 below, aligned to the right:\n\n   72\nx 63\n-----\n\nMultiply 72 by the 3 (ones place of 63):\n\n3 \u00d7 2 = 6, write down 6.\n\n3 \u00d7 7 = 21, write down 21 next to the 6, so the first partial product is 216.\n\nNow multiply 72 by the 6 (tens place of 63). But since it's the tens place, we shift one position to the left. So 6 \u00d7 2 = 12. Write down 2 and carry over 1.\n\n6 \u00d7 7 = 42 + 1 (carry) = 43. So the second partial product is 4320 (since shifted one left).\n\nNow add the two partial products:\n\n  216\n+4320\n------\n 4536\n\nSo that gives 4536. Which matches the second method where I broke it into (70+2)(60+3). Let me check that again:\n\n70\u00d760 = 4200\n\n70\u00d73 = 210\n\n2\u00d760 = 120\n\n2\u00d73 = 6\n\nAdding all: 4200 + 210 = 4410; 4410 + 120 = 4530; 4530 + 6 = 4536. Yep.\n\nSo where did I get 4896 earlier? Because I think I mixed up the numbers. Wait, in my first attempt I thought of 68\u00d756, which is a different problem. So I need to be careful here. The correct answer is 4536.\n\nAlternatively, I can also use the standard algorithm correctly now. So the steps are clear: 72\u00d73=216, 72\u00d760=4320, sum is 4536.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method like the lattice method, but that might take longer. Alternatively, break down 63 as 60+3, so 72\u00d760 is 4320, 72\u00d73 is 216, total 4536. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, use a calculator? But since I'm supposed to do it manually, but I think the answer is 4536.\n\nWait but in my first mistaken calculation where I did 6\u00d772 shifted, I got 4320, but then added 576? Wait, why 576? Wait, that was when I was multiplying by 8 instead of 3. So that was an error in the initial steps. So when I corrected myself, it's clear.\n\nTherefore, the product of 72 and 63 is 4536. I think that's correct. Let me verify with another approach.\n\nSuppose I do 72 \u00d7 60 = 4320, and 72 \u00d73 = 216. Adding those gives 4320 + 216.\n\n4320 + 200 = 4520, then +16 = 4536. Yep, that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, 63 \u00d770 = 4410 and 63\u00d72=126. So 4410 +126 = 4536. Same result.\n\nAlright, so I'm confident now that the answer is 4536.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 72 and 63 is \\boxed{4536}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 72 and 63, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Write down the numbers aligned to the right:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n    72 \\\\\n   \\times 63 \\\\\n   \\hline\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 72 by the ones place of 63 (which is 3):\n   \\[\n   72 \\times 3 = 216\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 72 by the tens place of 63 (which is 6, representing 60) and shift the result one position to the left:\n   \\[\n   72 \\times 60 = 4320\n   \\]\n\n4. Add the two partial products together:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n    216 \\\\\n   +4320 \\\\\n   \\hline\n    4536 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the product of 72 and 63 is \\(\\boxed{4536}\\).",
