nd for the tens place, making it 2200.\n\nNow add the two results: 165 + 2200 = 2365. Wait, that can't be right. Wait, hold on, 55 \u00d7 40 is 2200? Let me confirm. 55 times 4 is 220, so times 10 would be 2200, yes. Then 165 plus 2200 is 2365. But let me check another way.\n\nAlternatively, using the distributive property again: 55\u00d743 = (50 +5)(40 +3). \n\nBreaking it down:\n\n50\u00d740 = 2000\n\n50\u00d73 = 150\n\n5\u00d740 = 200\n\n5\u00d73 = 15\n\nAdding those together: 2000 + 150 = 2150; 2150 +200 = 2350; 2350 +15 = 2365. \n\nHmm, same result. But wait, maybe I should use another method to be sure. Let me do the multiplication vertically again carefully:\n\n55\n\n\u00d743\n\n--------\n\nFirst step: 43 \u00d7 5 (units place):\n\n43 \u00d75: 3\u00d75=15 (write down 5, carry 1), then 4\u00d75=20 plus 1=21. So 215.\n\nSecond step: 43 \u00d75 (tens place, so shifted one position left):\n\n43 \u00d750 (since it's the tens place). So 43 \u00d75 is 215, then add a zero to get 2150.\n\nNow add 215 and 2150: 215 + 2150 = 2365. \n\nSame answer. So perhaps that is correct. But let me verify with a calculator approach.\n\nAlternatively, 55 \u00d743. Let me compute 55\u00d740 = 2200, 55\u00d73=165. 2200+165=2365. Yep, consistent again.\n\nWait a minute, but earlier when I thought the question was 13\u00d777, I got confused and did the wrong problem. But now that I'm focusing on 55\u00d743, all methods are giving me 2365. Is that the correct answer?\n\nAlternatively, let me compute 55\u00d743 step by step:\n\n55 multiplied by 43:\n\nLet me think of 43 as 40 +3. So:\n\n55\u00d740 = 55\u00d74\u00d710 = (220)\u00d710 = 2200\n\n55\u00d73 = 165\n\nAdding together: 2200 + 165 = 2365. \n\nYes, that's correct. Alternatively, if I use a different breakdown, like 55 = 50 +5:\n\n(50 +5) \u00d743 = 50\u00d743 +5\u00d743\n\n50\u00d743 = 2150\n\n5\u00d743 = 215\n\n2150 +215 = 2365. \n\nSame answer. Hmm, so I think that's correct. \n\nWait, but just to make sure I didn't confuse numbers, let me try multiplying 43 by 55 in a different order. 43\u00d755.\n\n43\u00d750 = 2150, 43\u00d75=215, so again 2150 +215 = 2365. \n\nAlright, seems consistent. Maybe I was overcomplicating before when I first messed up. \n\nSo the correct answer should be 2365. I think that's right. Let me just do one more check with another method. For instance, using the lattice method or something else.\n\nAlternatively, consider that 55\u00d743 is the same as (50 +5)(40 +3). Wait, I already did that. \n\nAlternatively, 55 times 43 can be thought of as 55\u00d743 = 55\u00d7(50 -7). Because 50-7 is 43. \n\nSo 55\u00d750 = 2750 and 55\u00d77 = 385. Then subtract: 2750 -385 = 2365. \n\nYes, that works too. 2750 minus 385: 2750 - 300 is 2450, then minus 85 is 2365. Perfect. \n\nAlright, so after multiple methods and checks, I can confirm that 55 multiplied by 43 equals 2365. The earlier confusion was because I misread the problem initially, but upon correcting that, all methods converge to the same result. \n\nTherefore, the product of 55 and 43 is 2365.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 55 and 43 is calculated as follows:\n\nFirst, break down the multiplication using the distributive property:\n\\[\n55 \\times 43 = 55 \\times (40 + 3) = (55 \\times 40) + (55 \\times 3)\n\\]\n\nCalculate each part:\n\\[\n55 \\times 40 = 2200 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 55 \\times 3 = 165\n\\]\n\nAdd the results together:\n\\[\n2200 + 165 = 2365\n\\]\n\n**Verification:**\nUsing another method, multiply directly:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 55 \\\\\n\\times 43 \\\\\n\\hline\n   165 \\quad (\\text{55 \u00d7 3}) \\\\\n+2200 \\quad (\\text{55 \u00d7 40, shifted one position left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   2365 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the result is consistent.\n\n**Answer:** The product of 55 and 43 is \\boxed{2365}.",
