      "index": 0,
      "text": "10 times any number is that number with a zero added at the end. So 20 multiplied by 50 equals 200. Wait, that doesn't seem right. Let me check again.\n\nWait, no, maybe I made a mistake there. Let me recalculate. 54 times 10. Since 10 has one zero, multiplying by ten should just add a zero to the end of 54. So 54 becomes 540 when multiplied by 10. Yeah, that makes sense. But hold on, earlier I thought of 20 times 50 being 200, but that's actually 1000? No, 50 times 20 is 1000? Wait, no, 50 times 20 is 1000? Let me see: 50 times 10 is 500, so times 2 is 1000. But that's a different problem. The original question is 54 and 10. So maybe I confused numbers earlier, but the current problem is 54 multiplied by 10. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I should do it the standard way. Let me write it out. \n\n     54\n  \u00d7 10\n  -----\n     00 (which is 54 \u00d7 0)\n    54 (which is 54 \u00d7 10, shifted one position to the left)\n  -----\n    540\n\nYes, so adding those together gives 540. So the product is 540. Wait, that seems straightforward. But why did I get confused earlier? Maybe I was mixing up different problems. Let me confirm once more. \n\nAlternatively, using the place value method: 54 is 5 tens and 4 ones. Multiplying by 10 would increase each place value by one digit, so 54 \u00d7 10 becomes 540, which is 5 hundreds, 4 tens, and 0 ones. Yep, that checks out. \n\nAnother way: 10 is 10^1, so multiplying by 10 moves the decimal point one place to the right. Since 54 is a whole number, adding a zero at the end does the same thing. So 54.0 becomes 540.0. \n\nSo all methods point to the answer being 540. I think my initial confusion was because I started thinking of a different problem (20\u00d750) but then realized the actual question is 54\u00d710. So the correct answer should be 540. No more doubts here.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 54 and 10 is \\boxed{540}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 54 and 10, we can use the fact that multiplying any number by 10 simply appends a zero to the end of that number. \n\nStarting with the number 54, we multiply it by 10:\n\\[\n54 \\times 10 = 540\n\\]\n\nThis is verified by the standard multiplication method:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   54 \\\\\n\\times 10 \\\\\n\\hline\n   00 \\quad (\\text{54 \u00d7 0}) \\\\\n  540 \\quad (\\text{54 \u00d7 10, shifted one position to the left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n  540 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 54 and 10 is \\boxed{540}.",
