th diagonal (ten thousands): cell (1,1) TL (5)? Wait, no, perhaps not.\n\nWait, so adding these diagonals:\n\n- Units: 5\n\n- Tens:7\n\n- Hundreds:8\n\n- Thousands:7\n\nThus, the result is 7 (thousands), 8 (hundreds),7 (tens),5 (units) \u2192 7875? That can't be right, because we know the correct answer is 5985. There's a clear mistake here, so I must have misunderstood the lattice method setup.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the lattice is transposed? Wait, maybe I swapped rows and columns.\n\nWait, in the lattice method, the first number is on the top, and the second number is on the right. So multiplying 63 (top) by 95 (right), so the columns correspond to the digits of 63, rows to the digits of 95.\n\nWait, actually, perhaps I set up the rows and columns incorrectly. Let me check the standard setup:\n\nWhen multiplying a two-digit number (top) by another two-digit number (right), the grid has columns for the top number's digits and rows for the right number's digits. So if 63 is on top (digits 6 and 3), and 95 is on the right (digits 9 and 5), then the rows correspond to 9 and 5, and the columns to 6 and 3.\n\nHence, the grid should be:\n\nRows (for 9 and 5) and columns (for 6 and 3):\n\nFirst row (for digit 9):\n\nCells: (9\u00d76) and (9\u00d73)\n\nSecond row (for digit 5):\n\nCells: (5\u00d76) and (5\u00d73)\n\nWait, so my previous setup was reversed. I had rows for the digits of 63 and columns for 95, but it should be the other way.\n\nThis is likely where the error was. Let me correct the grid setup.\n\nCorrect Lattice Setup for 63 \u00d795:\n\n- The multiplicand (63) is split into digits 6 and 3 along the top.\n\n- The multiplier (95) is split into digits 9 and 5 along the right side.\n\n- The grid will then have rows for each digit of the multiplier (9 and 5) and columns for each digit of the multiplicand (6 and 3).\n\nThus, the grid is 2 rows (for 9 and 5) \u00d7 2 columns (for 6 and 3).\n\nSo:\n\nFirst row (top row corresponds to 9):\n\nCells:\n\nFirst column (6): 9\u00d76=54 \u2192 5|4\n\nSecond column (3):9\u00d73=27 \u21922|7\n\nSecond row (corresponding to 5):\n\nFirst column (6):5\u00d76=30 \u21923|0\n\nSecond column (3):5\u00d73=15 \u21921|5\n\nNow, arranging this grid:\n\nFirst row (9):\n\n| 5 4 | 2 7 |\n\nSecond row (5):\n\n| 3 0 | 1 5 |\n\nNow, the diagonals for summing are from bottom-left to top-right:\n\nStarting from the bottom-right corner (cell (2,2) which is 1|5):\n\nThe diagonals:\n\nFirst diagonal (units): the bottom-right part of the bottom-right cell (5).\n\nSecond diagonal (tens): the bottom-right of cell (2,1)=0 and the top-right of cell (2,2)'s top-left? Wait, let's see.\n\nWait, the diagonals now are different.\n\nLet me detail the cells:\n\nCells:\n\nRow1 (9), column1 (6): 5|4 (TL=5, BR=4)\n\nRow1 (9), column2 (3):2|7 (TL=2, BR=7)\n\nRow2 (5), column1 (6):3|0 (TL=3, BR=0)\n\nRow2 (5), column2 (3):1|5 (TL=1, BR=5)\n\nNow, the diagonals are as follows, starting from the bottom-right corner (cell (2,2)):\n\nFirst diagonal (units place): BR of cell (2,2) \u21925.\n\nSecond diagonal (tens place): BR of cell (2,1)=0 and BR of cell (1,2)=7 \u21920+7=7.\n\nThird diagonal (hundreds place): TL of cell (2,2)=1, TL of cell (1,2)=2, and BR of cell (1,1)=4 \u21921+2+4=7.\n\nFourth diagonal (thousands place): TL of cell (1,1)=5 and TL of cell (2,1)=3 \u21925+3=8.\n\nWait, that gives:\n\nThousands:8,\n\nHundreds:7,\n\nTens:7,\n\nUnits:5 \u2192 8775. Still not matching.\n\nHmm, I must be doing something wrong here.\n\nWait, the third diagonal should include:\n\nStarting from the bottom left of the grid:\n\nThe third diagonal would include:\n\nTL of cell (2,2)=1,\n\nTL of cell (1,2)=2,\n\nand BR of cell (1,1)=4?\n\nWait, maybe the diagonals are cumulative from right to left:\n\nFirst diagonal (units):5.\n\nSecond diagonal (tens):0 (from cell (2,1)'s BR) +7 (from cell (1,2)'s BR)=7.\n\nThird diagonal (hundreds):3 (cell (2,1)'s TL) +2 (cell (1,2)'s TL) +4 (cell (1,1)'s BR)=3+2+4=9.\n\nFourth diagonal (thousands):5 (cell (1,1)'s TL) +1 (cell (2,2)'s TL)=6? Wait no:\n\nWait, cell (2,2)'s TL is 1, cell (1,1)'s TL is5. So 5+1=6?\n\nWait, this is very confusing. Perhaps I need to visualize the diagonals properly.\n\nLet me arrange the grid with coordinates:\n\nThe grid has four cells:\n\n(1,1):5|4 (row1, column1)\n\n(1,2):2|7 (row1, column2)\n\n(2,1):3|0 (row2, column1)\n\n(2,2):1|5 (row2, column2)\n\nNow, the diagonals to add are from bottom-left to top-right:\n\nStarting from the bottom-left corner (cell (2,1)):\n\n- The first diagonal (units place) is at the far right, which is cell (2,2)'s BR:5.\n\n- Moving to the left diagonally:\n\nSecond diagonal (tens place):\n\nFrom cell (2,1)'s BR (0) and cell (1,2)'s BR (7). So 0 +7=7.\n\nThird diagonal (hundreds place):\n\nFrom cell (1,1)'s BR (4), cell (2,1)'s TL (3), and cell (1,2)'s TL (2). So 4+3+2=9.\n\nFourth diagonal (thousands place):\n\nFrom cell (1,1)'s TL (5) and cell (2,2)'s TL (1). Wait, but those are on separate diagonals?\n\nAlternatively, the thousands place would be cell (1,1)'s TL (5) and cell (2,2)'s TL (1) summed? No, perhaps the fourth diagonal is only cell (1,1)'s TL=5, and the fifth diagonal cell (2,2)'s TL=1? That would make it 5 (thousands) and 1 (ten thousands?) but that complicates.\n\nAlternatively, the diagonals are:\n\n- Units:5\n\n- Tens:7\n\n- Hundreds:9\n\n- Thousands:3 (from cell (2,1)'s TL=3) +5 (cell (1,1)'s TL=5) \u21928?\n\nWait, no, this is getting too convoluted. It's clear that my lattice method is error-prone here because of confusion in setup. Since other methods confirm 5985, I'll trust those.\n\nTherefore, returning to the original problem: 63 multiplied by 95 is 5985.\n\nBut just to make sure, let's do another verification. Let's use the standard algorithm again:\n\n63\n\n\u00d795\n\n--------\n\nFirst: 63\u00d75=315\n\nSecond:63\u00d790=5670\n\nAdding 315 +5670:\n\n315 +5670:\n\n315 +5000=5315\n\n5315 +670=5985. Yes, correct.\n\nAlternatively, using another multiplier breakdown:\n\n63\u00d795 = (60 +3)(90 +5) = 60\u00d790 +60\u00d75 +3\u00d790 +3\u00d75 =5400+300+270+15=5400+300=5700; 5700+270=5970; 5970+15=5985. Correct.\n\nAlright, so the correct product is 5985. The earlier confusion was due to my initial misreading of the problem (thought it was 84\u00d789), then during the lattice method, I messed up the setup, which led to inconsistency. But after verifying with multiple methods, it's clear the answer is 5985.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 63 and 95 is \\boxed{5985}.\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem of finding the product of 63 and 95, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. **Multiply 63 by the units digit of 95 (which is 5):**\n   \\[\n   63 \\times 5 = 315\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 63 by the tens digit of 95 (which is 9, representing 90):**\n   \\[\n   63 \\times 90 = 63 \\times 9 \\times 10 = 567 \\times 10 = 5670\n   \\]\n\n3. **Add the two partial products:**\n   \\[\n   315 + 5670 = 5985\n   \\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the distributive property:\n\\[\n63 \\times 95 = 63 \\times (100 - 5) = 63 \\times 100 - 63 \\times 5 = 6300 - 315 = 5985\n\\]\n\nAnother verification using the distributive property:\n\\[\n63 \\times 95 = (60 + 3) \\times (90 + 5) = 60 \\times 90 + 60 \\times 5 + 3 \\times 90 + 3 \\times 5\n\\]\n\\[\n= 5400 + 300 + 270 + 15 = 5400 + 300 = 5700; \\quad 5700 + 270 = 5970; \\quad 5970 + 15 = 5985\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 63 and 95 is \\boxed{5985}.",
