rect.\n\nWait, in my initial wrong problem, I had 25\u00d715, but the user's question is 93\u00d766. So my final correct answer is 6,138. I need to make sure I didn't get confused in the middle steps.\n\nWait, in my first lines, the user's question is product of 93 and 66. But when I started thinking, I wrote \"What is the Product of 25 and 15?\" That was a mistake. Probably a typo when I was starting to type, but the user's actual question is correct. So I need to focus on 93\u00d766.\n\nTherefore, after correcting my initial mistake, the correct product is 6,138.\n\nBut let me try another method to confirm, like the lattice method.\n\nSetting up a lattice for 93 \u00d766:\n\nSince both numbers are two digits, the lattice will be 2x2.\n\nDigits of 93: 9 (tens) and 3 (units)\n\nDigits of 66: 6 (tens) and 6 (units)\n\nThe lattice grid:\n\n       6      6\n    -----------------\n9 | 9\u00d76=54  9\u00d76=54\n3 | 3\u00d76=18  3\u00d76=18\n\nWait, actually, each cell is the product of the row digit and column digit.\n\nSo arranging it properly:\n\nColumns are the multiplier (66), so first column is 6 (tens place), second column 6 (units).\n\nRows are the multiplicand (93): first row 9 (tens), second row 3 (units).\n\nEach cell is split by a diagonal.\n\nFirst cell (row 9, column 6): 9\u00d76=54 \u2192 write 5 on the left, 4 on the right.\n\nSecond cell (row 9, column 6): 9\u00d76=54 again.\n\nThird cell (row 3, column 6): 3\u00d76=18 \u2192 1 and 8.\n\nFourth cell (row 3, column 6): 3\u00d76=18 again.\n\nWait, maybe I need to structure it properly. Let me see:\n\nActually, the lattice grid should be set up with the multiplicand on the top and the multiplier on the right. Wait, maybe I got rows and columns reversed. Let me recall:\n\nIn lattice multiplication, the multiplicand is on the top, and the multiplier on the right side. Each cell is divided by a diagonal. The digits of the multiplicand are on the top, and the digits of the multiplier on the right.\n\nSo for 93 \u00d7 66,\n\nTop: 9 and 3 (the multiplicand digits)\n\nRight side: 6 and 6 (the multiplier digits, with the first 6 being the tens place and the second 6 the units? Wait, actually, the right side is written from bottom to top, corresponding to the multiplier digits.\n\nWait, maybe it's better to draw it step by step.\n\nBut perhaps this is getting too time-consuming. Alternatively, trust that the two previous methods have confirmed it's 6,138.\n\nAlternatively, use another verification: 93 \u00d766 = (90 + 3)(60 +6)= as done before, gives 6,138.\n\nAlternatively, use the fact that 93 \u00d766 = 93\u00d7(70 -4) = 93\u00d770 - 93\u00d74.\n\nCompute 93\u00d770: 93\u00d77\u00d710. 93\u00d77: 90\u00d77=630, 3\u00d77=21 \u2192 651. So 651\u00d710=6,510.\n\nThen subtract 93\u00d74: 93\u00d74= 372.\n\nSo 6,510 - 372 = 6,138. Correct again.\n\nAlright, so multiple methods confirm that the product of 93 and 66 is 6,138.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 6,138.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 93 and 66 is calculated as follows:\n\n**Step-by-Step Calculation:**\n1. **Break down the numbers using the distributive property:**\n   \\[\n   93 \\times 66 = (90 + 3) \\times (60 + 6)\n   \\]\n2. **Expand the terms:**\n   \\[\n   = (90 \\times 60) + (90 \\times 6) + (3 \\times 60) + (3 \\times 6)\n   \\]\n3. **Calculate each part:**\n   - \\(90 \\times 60 = 5,\\!400\\)\n   - \\(90 \\times 6 = 540\\)\n   - \\(3 \\times 60 = 180\\)\n   - \\(3 \\times 6 = 18\\)\n4. **Sum the results:**\n   \\[\n   5,\\!400 + 540 + 180 + 18 = 6,\\!138\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe product of 93 and 66 is \\(\\boxed{6138}\\).",
