      "index": 0,
      "text": "20000.\n\nWait a second, that doesn't seem right. Let me check my calculations again. Maybe I made a mistake in the partial products.\n\nLet me try a different approach using the distributive property. Break down 32 into 60 + 9, and 47 into 50 + 7. Then multiply each part:\n\n(60 + 9) \u00d7 (50 + 7) = 60\u00d750 + 60\u00d77 + 9\u00d750 + 9\u00d77.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n60\u00d750 = 3000,\n\n60\u00d77 = 420,\n\n9\u00d750 = 450,\n\n9\u00d77 = 63.\n\nNow add them all together: 3000 + 420 = 3420,\n\n3420 + 450 = 3870,\n\n3870 + 63 = 3933.\n\nHmm, that gives me 3933, but earlier I got 20000. There's a discrepancy here. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the first method's place values. Let me go back to the first method and see.\n\nOriginal numbers: 57 and 69.\n\nFirst partial product: 57 \u00d7 9. 57\u00d79: 50\u00d79=450 and 7\u00d79=63, so 450+63=513. That's correct.\n\nSecond partial product: 57 \u00d7 60. Since the 6 is in the tens place, it's 60. So 57\u00d760. 57\u00d76=342, so \u00d710 gives 3420.\n\nAdding 513 + 3420 gives 3933. Okay, so my first method had an error in the partial products. Probably added wrong before. So the correct answer is 3933.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the standard algorithm again:\n\n\u200357\n\u00d769\n-----\nFirst, multiply 57 by 9: 513.\n\nThen multiply 57 by 60: 3420.\n\nAdding them gives 513 + 3420 = 3933. Yes, that's consistent with the distributive method.\n\nSo the product of 57 and 69 is 3933. Let me verify with another method just to be sure.\n\nUsing the formula (a + b)(c + d) again, but breaking down differently. Let's see:\n\n57 = 50 + 7,\n\n69 = 60 + 9.\n\nSo (50 +7)(60 +9) = 50\u00d760 +50\u00d79 +7\u00d760 +7\u00d79 = 3000 +450 +420 +63 = same as before, totals 3933.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using a calculator? Well, since I can't use a calculator here, but let me do 57\u00d770 first, which is 57\u00d77\u00d710. 57\u00d77 is 399, so 399\u00d710=3990. Then subtract 57 to get 57\u00d769: 3990 -57=3933. That works too.\n\nSo all methods point to 3933. The initial mistake was probably an arithmetic error in adding or multiplying during the first attempt. So the correct answer is 3933.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 57 and 69 is \\boxed{3933}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 57 and 69, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm and the distributive property for verification.\n\n**Using the standard multiplication algorithm:**\n\n1. Multiply 57 by 9 (the rightmost digit of 69):\n   \\[\n   57 \\times 9 = 513\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 57 by 60 (the tens place digit of 69):\n   \\[\n   57 \\times 60 = 3420\n   \\]\n3. Add the partial products together:\n   \\[\n   513 + 3420 = 3933\n   \\]\n\n**Using the distributive property:**\n\n1. Break down the numbers into their place values:\n   \\[\n   57 = 50 + 7 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 69 = 60 + 9\n   \\]\n2. Apply the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   (50 + 7)(60 + 9) = 50 \\times 60 + 50 \\times 9 + 7 \\times 60 + 7 \\times 9\n   \\]\n3. Calculate each term:\n   \\[\n   50 \\times 60 = 3000\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   50 \\times 9 = 450\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   7 \\times 60 = 420\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   7 \\times 9 = 63\n   \\]\n4. Sum all the terms:\n   \\[\n   3000 + 450 + 420 + 63 = 3933\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 57 and 69 is \\(\\boxed{3933}\\).",
