      "text": "238. Wait, that doesn't seem right. Wait, hold on, maybe I messed up the multiplication steps.\n\nLet me check again. Maybe I need to slow down and do each digit step by step.\n\nWait, actually, perhaps I made a mistake in the alignment. Let me try writing this out again more carefully.\n\nLet me write 18 on top and 88 below. So:\n\n\u2003\u200318\n\u00d7\u00a088\n-------\n.\n\nStarting with the ones place first. The bottom number's ones digit is 8. Multiply 17 by 8.\n\nSo:\n\n17 \u00d7 8:\n\n7 \u00d7 8 = 56. Write down 6, carry over 5.\n\n1 \u00d7 8 = 8, plus the carried 5 = 13. So that gives 136.\n\nNow, moving to the tens place. The bottom number's next digit is 8 (but since it's the tens place, it's actually 80). So multiply 17 by 80. But since it's 80, we can do 17\u00d78\u00d710. Let me do 17\u00d78 first, which we know is 136, so 136\u00d710 is 1360.\n\nNow, add the two results together: 136 + 1360 = 1496.\n\nWait, that seems better. So 17\u00d780 is 1360 plus 136 gives 1496. Hmm, but wait, the original question was 17 and 82. Wait, hold on! Wait, the user asked for the product of 17 and 82, but in my thinking process, I started with 86 and 93, then switched to 66\u00d727, then 30\u00d769, then 11\u00d759, then 18\u00d788, but in the problem statement, it's 17 and 82. Did I get confused here?\n\nOh no, I must have mixed up the numbers. Let me start over. The user's question is: \"What is the Product of 17 and 82?\" So I need to compute 17 \u00d7 82. I think I might have been solving a different problem in my head earlier, so I need to correct that.\n\nAlright, let's focus on 17 multiplied by 82. Let's do this step by step properly.\n\nFirst, write the numbers vertically aligned:\n\n\u2003\u00a017\n\u00d7\u00a082\n-------\n.\n\nStarting with the ones digit of the bottom number, which is 2. Multiply 17 by 2:\n\n7\u00d72 = 14. Write down 4, carry over 1.\n\n1\u00d72 = 2, plus the carried 1 is 3. So first partial product is 34.\n\nNext, multiply by the tens digit of the bottom number, which is 8 (remember, this is actually 80, so the result will be shifted left by one position). So 17\u00d78:\n\n7\u00d78 = 56. Write down 6, carry over 5.\n\n1\u00d78 = 8, plus the carried 5 is 13. So the second partial product is 136, but shifted one place to the left, so it becomes 1360.\n\nNow, add the two partial products:\n\n\u2003\u00a034\n+1360\n-------\n\u00a01394\n\nWait, 34 plus 1360 is 1394. Hmm, let me check if that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can compute it another way to verify. Let's try the distributive property: 17\u00d782 = 17\u00d7(80 + 2) = 17\u00d780 + 17\u00d72.\n\n17\u00d780: 17\u00d78=136, so 136\u00d710=1360.\n\n17\u00d72=34.\n\nAdding them gives 1360 + 34 = 1394. So that checks out. So the answer should be 1394.\n\nWait, but let me try another method for confirmation. Maybe breaking down the numbers further.\n\nAlternatively, 82 is 80 + 2, so 17\u00d780 is straightforward as before. Alternatively, 17\u00d782 can be thought of as (20 - 3)\u00d782. Hmm, 20\u00d782=1640, minus 3\u00d782=246. 1640 - 246: 1640 - 200 = 1440, then subtract 46 more gives 1394. That matches.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using the standard algorithm again but being extra careful:\n\nMultiplying 17 by 82:\n\nFirst, 2\u00d717 = 34.\n\nThen, 80\u00d717: 17\u00d78=136, so 136\u00d710 = 1360.\n\nAdding 34 + 1360: yes, that's 1394.\n\nAlternatively, if I had done 82\u00d717, same result. Let's see:\n\n82\u00d710 is 820,\n\n82\u00d77 is 574,\n\nAdding those: 820 + 574 = 1394. Yep, same answer.\n\nHmm, so I think that's correct. Wait, but in the initial steps, I was confused because I started with different numbers, but now focusing on 17\u00d782, I get 1394.\n\nWait, but let me just do the multiplication step by step again, just to be absolutely sure. Sometimes when we do mental math, we can slip up.\n\nSo, writing it out:\n\n\u2003\u2003\u200317\n\u2003\u00d7\u00a082\n\u2003-------\n\u2003\u2003\u00a034 \u00a0\u2190 17 \u00d7 2\n\u20031360 \u2190 17 \u00d7 80 (shifted one position to the left)\n-------\n\u20031394\n\nYes, that seems right. The addition of 34 and 1360 is indeed 1394. Therefore, the product of 17 and 82 is 1394.\n\nAlternatively, to ensure there's no mistake, let me do the multiplication in another way. Let's use the lattice method or another technique.\n\nAlternatively, 17\u00d782. Let me compute 17\u00d780 first:\n\n17\u00d780: 10\u00d780=800, 7\u00d780=560, so total is 800+560=1360.\n\nThen 17\u00d72=34.\n\nAdding them gives 1360 +34=1394.\n\nAnother way: 17\u00d782.\n\nLet me compute 17\u00d782 as follows:\n\n17\u00d782 = (10 +7) \u00d782 = 10\u00d782 +7\u00d782.\n\n10\u00d782 is 820,\n\n7\u00d782: 7\u00d780=560 and 7\u00d72=14, so 560+14=574.\n\nAdding 820 +574: 820 + 500 is 1320, plus 74 is 1394. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, use the standard vertical multiplication again but perhaps with more detailed steps:\n\nMultiplying 17 by 2 (units place):\n\n7\u00d72=14, write down 4, carry over 1.\n\n1\u00d72=2 plus 1 is 3. So first line is 34.\n\nThen multiplying by 8 (tens place, so actually 80):\n\nStart by putting a zero in the units place for the next line (since we're moving to tens).\n\nWait, actually, in the standard method, you don't write the zero, but shift the result. So when multiplying by the tens digit (8), you can shift the result one place to the left.\n\nSo 7\u00d78=56, write 6, carryover 5.\n\n1\u00d78=8 plus 5 is 13. So that gives 136, but shifted left one digit: 1360.\n\nAdding 34 +1360 gives 1394.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check with a calculator, but since I don't have one here, I'll just do the addition again:\n\n34 +1360:\n\n34 +1360. 34 +1300=1334, plus 60 is 1394.\n\nOkay, so all methods lead to 1394. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 17 and 82 is 1394.\n\nWait, but let me check once more with another method. Let's use the distributive law in another way:\n\n82 can be written as 80 + 2,\n\n17 can be written as 20 - 3.\n\nSo, 17\u00d782 = (20 -3)(80 +2).\n\nMultiply this out:\n\n20\u00d780 + 20\u00d72 -3\u00d780 -3\u00d72\n\n= 1600 +40 -240 -6\n\nCalculate each term:\n\n1600 +40 = 1640\n\n1640 -240 = 1400 - wait, 1640 -240: 1640 - 200 = 1440, minus 40 more is 1400. Hmm, but then minus 6 gives 1394. Yep.\n\nSo that also gives 1394. So that's another confirmation.\n\nTherefore, after verifying through multiple methods\u2014standard multiplication, distributive property, breaking into components, and cross-checking\u2014I can confirm that the product is indeed 1394.\n\nI think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations now. Initially, I got confused because I started with different numbers but realized that and corrected myself. So the final answer should be 1394.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 17 and 82 is \\boxed{1394}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 17 and 82, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Write the numbers vertically aligned:\n   ```\n    17\n  \u00d7 82\n  -------\n  ```\n\n2. Multiply 17 by the ones digit of 82 (which is 2):\n   ```\n    17\n  \u00d7  2\n  -------\n    34  (since 17 \u00d7 2 = 34)\n  ```\n\n3. Multiply 17 by the tens digit of 82 (which is 8, representing 80), and shift the result one position to the left:\n   ```\n    17\n  \u00d7 80\n  -------\n   1360  (since 17 \u00d7 8 = 136, and 136 \u00d7 10 = 1360)\n  ```\n\n4. Add the two partial products together:\n   ```\n     34\n + 1360\n -------\n   1394\n  ```\n\nVerification using the distributive property:\n- \\( 17 \\times 82 = 17 \\times (80 + 2) = 17 \\times 80 + 17 \\times 2 \\)\n- \\( 17 \\times 80 = 1360 \\)\n- \\( 17 \\times 2 = 34 \\)\n- Adding these results: \\( 1360 + 34 = 1394 \\)\n\nAnother verification using different decomposition:\n- \\( 17 \\times 82 = (20 - 3) \\times 82 \\)\n- \\( 20 \\times 82 = 1640 \\)\n- \\( 3 \\times 82 = 246 \\)\n- Subtracting these results: \\( 1640 - 246 = 1394 \\)\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 17 and 82 is \\(\\boxed{1394}\\).",
