ion is 53 and 94. So maybe I got confused there. But the standard method and the distributive method both give 4982, so that's the right answer. Just need to make sure I didn't make any arithmetic errors in those steps.\n\nAlternatively, another way to verify is using the calculator, but since I'm doing this manually, let me try another approach. Let's compute 53 \u00d7 94 as follows: 53 \u00d7 100 would be 5300, but since it's 94, which is 100 - 6, so 53\u00d7(100 - 6) = 53\u00d7100 - 53\u00d76 = 5300 - 318 = 5300 - 300 = 5000, then subtract 18 more: 5000 - 18 = 4982. That also works. \n\nYes, that's another way and it also gives 4982. So I'm confident that's correct. Therefore, the product of 53 and 94 is 4,982.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 53 and 94 is \\boxed{4982}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 53 and 94, we can use the distributive property and break down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n1. Break down 94 into 90 and 4:\n   \\[\n   53 \\times 94 = 53 \\times (90 + 4)\n   \\]\n2. Apply the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   53 \\times 90 + 53 \\times 4\n   \\]\n3. Calculate each term separately:\n   - \\(53 \\times 90\\):\n     \\[\n     53 \\times 9 = 477 \\quad \\text{then add a zero} \\quad 4770\n     \\]\n   - \\(53 \\times 4\\):\n     \\[\n     53 \\times 4 = 212\n     \\]\n4. Add the results of the two terms:\n   \\[\n   4770 + 212 = 4982\n   \\]\n\nAnother method using the formula \\((a + b)(c + d)\\):\n1. Break down both numbers into tens and units:\n   \\[\n   (50 + 3)(90 + 4)\n   \\]\n2. Expand the product:\n   \\[\n   50 \\times 90 + 50 \\times 4 + 3 \\times 90 + 3 \\times 4\n   \\]\n3. Calculate each term:\n   - \\(50 \\times 90 = 4500\\)\n   - \\(50 \\times 4 = 200\\)\n   - \\(3 \\times 90 = 270\\)\n   - \\(3 \\times 4 = 12\\)\n4. Sum all the terms:\n   \\[\n   4500 + 200 + 270 + 12 = 4982\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 53 and 94 is \\(\\boxed{4982}\\).",
