      "text": "6201.\n\nWait, that doesn't seem right. Let me check my steps again. Maybe I made a mistake in the multiplication steps.\n\nStarting over, 89 \u00d7 61. Breaking it down using distributive property: (80 + 5) \u00d7 (60 + 1). Wait, no, actually, 89 is 80 + 9, and 61 is 60 + 1. So expanding that gives 80\u00d760 + 80\u00d71 + 9\u00d760 + 9\u00d71.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n80\u00d760 = 4,800.\n\n80\u00d71 = 80.\n\n9\u00d760 = 540.\n\n9\u00d71 = 9.\n\nAdding them together: 4,800 + 80 = 4,880; 4,880 + 540 = 5,420; 5,420 + 9 = 5,429. Hmm, that gives me 5,429. But earlier I had 6201, which is different. So clearly, I messed up the previous calculation.\n\nLet me try the standard algorithm again, more carefully.\n\nMultiplying 89 by 61.\n\nWrite:\n\n\u200389.\n\n\u00d761.\n\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 89 by the ones digit of 61, which is 1: 89 \u00d7 1 = 89.\n\nNext, multiply 89 by the tens digit, which is 6, but since it's in the tens place, it's 60. So 89 \u00d7 60.\n\nCalculating 89 \u00d7 60: 89 \u00d7 6 \u00d7 10.\n\n89 \u00d7 6: 80\u00d76=480, 9\u00d76=54; 480+54=534. So 534 \u00d710 = 5,340.\n\nNow add the two partial products:\n\n\u200389.\n\n+5,340.\n\n= 5,429.\n\nOkay, so that matches the distributive method. So my first calculation was wrong because I must have multiplied incorrectly. Probably messed up the digits when I did the first method. So the correct answer is 5,429.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method, like the lattice method? Let me try that to confirm.\n\nBut perhaps it's faster to do another check with addition. Since 89 \u00d7 60 is 5,340, adding another 89 gives 5,340 + 89 = 5,429. Yep, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, using the standard algorithm again:\n\n\u200389.\n\n\u00d761.\n\n--------\n\nMultiply 1 (units place) \u00d7 89 = 89.\n\nMultiply 6 (tens place, so actually 60) \u00d789 = 5,340.\n\nAdding them gives 89 +5,340=5,429.\n\nTherefore, the product is 5,429.\n\nWait, but the original question was 89 and 61. I think that's correct. But just to make sure, maybe approximate?\n\n89 is close to 90, and 61 is close to 60. 90\u00d760=5,400. The actual numbers are a bit less (since 89 is 1 less than 90, and 61 is 1 more than 60), so the product should be slightly less than 5,400? Wait, no, because 89 is 1 less, but 61 is 1 more, so the difference might offset. Let's see:\n\nThe exact difference: 90\u00d760 = 5,400.\n\nBut we have (90-1)*(60+1) = 90\u00d760 +90\u00d71 -1\u00d760 -1\u00d71 =5,400 +90 -60 -1=5,400+29=5,429.\n\nExactly. So that's the same as before. Therefore, 5,429 is correct. So my first calculation was wrong because I must have miscalculated the multiplication steps. Probably a place value error. So the correct answer is 5,429.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 89 and 61 is \\boxed{5429}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 89 and 61, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Multiply 89 by the ones digit of 61 (which is 1):\n   \\[\n   89 \\times 1 = 89\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 89 by the tens digit of 61 (which is 6, representing 60):\n   \\[\n   89 \\times 60 = 89 \\times 6 \\times 10\n   \\]\n   Breaking it down:\n   \\[\n   89 \\times 6 = 534 \\quad \\text{(since } 80 \\times 6 = 480 \\text{ and } 9 \\times 6 = 54\\text{)}\n   \\]\n   Then multiply by 10:\n   \\[\n   534 \\times 10 = 5340\n   \\]\n\n3. Add the two partial products together:\n   \\[\n   89 + 5340 = 5429\n   \\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the distributive property:\n\\[\n(80 + 9) \\times (60 + 1) = 80 \\times 60 + 80 \\times 1 + 9 \\times 60 + 9 \\times 1\n\\]\nCalculating each term:\n\\[\n80 \\times 60 = 4800, \\quad 80 \\times 1 = 80, \\quad 9 \\times 60 = 540, \\quad 9 \\times 1 = 9\n\\]\nAdding these together:\n\\[\n4800 + 80 + 540 + 9 = 5429\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 89 and 61 is \\boxed{5429}.",
