 1*60. 40*60 is 2400, minus 60 is 2340. That also gives the same result. \n\nAlright, so all methods lead to 2340. I think that's solid. The initial confusion was my own mistake in the thought process, but after verifying with different methods, the answer is definitely 2340.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 39 and 60 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Breakdown Method**:  \n   Recognize that multiplying by 60 is the same as multiplying by 6 and then by 10.  \n   - First, compute \\( 39 \\times 6 \\):  \n     \\( 30 \\times 6 = 180 \\)  \n     \\( 9 \\times 6 = 54 \\)  \n     Adding these: \\( 180 + 54 = 234 \\).  \n   - Then multiply by 10:  \n     \\( 234 \\times 10 = 2340 \\).  \n\n2. **Long Multiplication**:  \n   - Multiply 39 by 0 (units place of 60):  \n     \\( 39 \\times 0 = 0 \\).  \n   - Multiply 39 by 6 (tens place of 60, representing 60):  \n     \\( 39 \\times 6 = 234 \\).  \n   - Shift the result one place to the left (equivalent to multiplying by 10):  \n     \\( 234 \\times 10 = 2340 \\).  \n\n3. **Distributive Property**:  \n   Rewrite \\( 39 \\times 60 \\) as \\( (40 - 1) \\times 60 \\):  \n   - \\( 40 \\times 60 = 2400 \\)  \n   - \\( 1 \\times 60 = 60 \\)  \n   - Subtract: \\( 2400 - 60 = 2340 \\).  \n\nAll methods confirm the result is **2340**.\n\n**:boxed{2340}**",
