00 is 75. So 75*15 = (3/4 *100)*15 = (3/4)*1500. 1500 divided by 4 is 375, times 3 is 1125. Hmm, that's another way.\n\nAlternatively, using exponents? Maybe not necessary here. All methods seem to lead to 1125. Let me check with a calculator method.\n\nAlternatively, do 75*15:\n\n75*10 =750, 75*5=375, so total 750+375=1125.\n\nYes, that's correct. So the product is 1125. Therefore, the answer should be 1125. I think that's solid. Let me just confirm once more.\n\nWait, in my initial thought process, I had some confusion with different numbers, but focusing now on 75 and 15, I can't see any errors in these calculations. All the different methods give the same result, so I think that's correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 75 and 15 is \\boxed{1125}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 75 and 15, we can use the distributive property and standard multiplication methods:\n\n1. **Breaking down the multiplication**:\n   - Calculate \\( 75 \\times 10 \\):\n     \\[\n     75 \\times 10 = 750\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\( 75 \\times 5 \\):\n     \\[\n     75 \\times 5 = 375\n     \\]\n   - Add the results of the two calculations:\n     \\[\n     750 + 375 = 1125\n     \\]\n\n2. **Using the standard algorithm**:\n   - Multiply 75 by 5:\n     \\[\n     75 \\times 5 = 375\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 75 by 10 (since the 1 in 15 is in the tens place, this is equivalent to 75 multiplied by 10):\n     \\[\n     75 \\times 10 = 750\n     \\]\n   - Add the results of the two calculations:\n     \\[\n     375 + 750 = 1125\n     \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 75 and 15 is \\(\\boxed{1125}\\).",
