g it step by step, it's definitely 726. \n\nWait, but in my first attempt I thought of 65 and 18? Maybe I was mixing up numbers. Let me make sure. The question is 22 and 33. So 22 \u00d7 33. \n\nAlternatively, another way to check is using a calculator, but since I can't do that here, I'll stick with the step-by-step. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using exponents? 22 is 2\u00d711, 33 is 3\u00d711. So 22\u00d733 = (2\u00d73) \u00d7 (11\u00d711) = 6 \u00d7 121 = 726. That also works. \n\nYes, 11\u00d711 is 121, multiplied by 6 gives 726. That confirms it again. \n\nSo, the correct answer should be 726. My initial confusion was because I thought I was solving a different problem, but upon careful step-by-step, it's clear that 22 multiplied by 33 is indeed 726.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 22 and 33 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Breakdown using distributive property**:\n   \\[\n   22 \\times 33 = (20 + 2) \\times (30 + 3)\n   \\]\n   Expand the terms:\n   \\[\n   = 20 \\times 30 + 20 \\times 3 + 2 \\times 30 + 2 \\times 3\n   \\]\n   Calculate each part:\n   \\[\n   = 600 + 60 + 60 + 6 = 726\n   \\]\n\n2. **Standard multiplication**:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     \\quad 22 \\\\\n   \\times 33 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     \\quad 66 \\quad (\\text{22 \u00d7 3}) \\\\\n   + 660 \\quad (\\text{22 \u00d7 30, shifted left}) \\\\\n   \\hline\n     726 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Prime factorization**:\n   \\[\n   22 = 2 \\times 11,\\quad 33 = 3 \\times 11 \\\\\n   22 \\times 33 = (2 \\times 3) \\times (11 \\times 11) = 6 \\times 121 = 726\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe product of 22 and 33 is \\boxed{726}.",
