g it 980.\n\nAdding 147 and 980 together: 147 + 980. Let's do this carefully. 147 + 900 is 1047, and then +80 is 1127. So the total is 1127.\n\nAlternatively, using another method like the distributive property: 49\u00d723 = 49\u00d7(20 + 3) = (49\u00d720) + (49\u00d73) = 980 + 147 = 1127. Yep, same result.\n\nI think that's solid. Let me check with a calculator in my head another way. Maybe breaking down 49 into 50-1. So (50 - 1) \u00d723 = 50\u00d723 - 1\u00d723. 50\u00d723 is 1150, subtract 23 gives 1150 -23 = 1127. Perfect, same answer.\n\nAnother cross-verification: 23\u00d750 is 1150, subtract 23 (since it's 23\u00d749) gives 1150-23=1127. Yep.\n\nTherefore, I'm confident that the product of 49 and 23 is 1127.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 49 and 23 is \\boxed{1127}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 49 and 23, we can use the standard multiplication method:\n\n1. Multiply 49 by 3 (the ones place of 23):\n   \\[\n   49 \\times 3 = 147\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 49 by 20 (the tens place of 23, which is 2 shifted one position to the left):\n   \\[\n   49 \\times 20 = 980\n   \\]\n3. Add the results of the two multiplications:\n   \\[\n   147 + 980 = 1127\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this using the distributive property:\n\\[\n49 \\times 23 = 49 \\times (20 + 3) = (49 \\times 20) + (49 \\times 3) = 980 + 147 = 1127\n\\]\n\nAnother verification using the distributive property with 50 - 1:\n\\[\n(50 - 1) \\times 23 = 50 \\times 23 - 1 \\times 23 = 1150 - 23 = 1127\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 49 and 23 is \\(\\boxed{1127}\\).",
