30. Breaking down 23 into 20 + 3 and 30 into 30.\n\nSo, (20 + 3) \u00d7 30 = 20\u00d730 + 3\u00d730 = 600 + 90 = 690. Yep, same answer. \n\nAlright, I think that's solid. All different methods lead to 690, so I feel confident that's the right product.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 23 and 30 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Breakdown Method**:  \n   Recognize that 30 can be expressed as \\(3 \\times 10\\).  \n   \\(23 \\times 30 = 23 \\times (3 \\times 10)\\)  \n   First compute \\(23 \\times 3 = 69\\), then multiply by 10:  \n   \\(69 \\times 10 = 690\\).\n\n2. **Standard Multiplication**:  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     \\quad 23 \\\\\n   \\times 30 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     \\quad 0 \\quad (\\text{23 \u00d7 0}) \\\\\n   + 690 \\quad (\\text{23 \u00d7 30, shifted left by one digit}) \\\\\n   \\hline\n     \\quad 690 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Distributive Property**:  \n   \\(23 \\times 30 = 23 \\times (20 + 10) = (23 \\times 20) + (23 \\times 10) = 460 + 230 = 690\\).\n\n**Final Answer**:  \nThe product of 23 and 30 is \\(\\boxed{690}\\).",
