-right to bottom-left. So each diagonal is formed by the bottom-right parts of the cells.\n\nLet me try to outline the diagonals:\n\nThe first diagonal (units place) is the bottom-right of the bottom-right cell: 2.\n\nSecond diagonal (tens place) is the bottom-right of the bottom-left cell (4) and the top-right of the bottom-right cell (0). Wait, perhaps I need to think of it as the diagonal line cutting through the cells.\n\nAlternatively, here's a better way:\n\nAfter filling the grid with the products split by diagonals, you add the numbers along each diagonal starting from the rightmost diagonal.\n\nThe grid would look like this:\n\nFor the top-left cell (28):\n\nTop part: 2\n\nBottom part:8\n\nSplit by a diagonal line.\n\nSimilarly, top-right cell (14): top 1, bottom 4.\n\nBottom-left cell (4): top 0, bottom 4.\n\nBottom-right cell (2): top 0, bottom 2.\n\nNow, the diagonals to add:\n\nThe rightmost diagonal is the bottom part of the bottom-right cell: 2.\n\nNext diagonal to the left: the bottom part of the top-right cell (4) and the bottom part of the bottom-left cell (4). Wait, no, perhaps:\n\nActually, the diagonals are formed by the diagonal lines in the grid. The first diagonal (units place) is the bottom-right corner of the grid, which is the bottom part of the bottom-right cell (2). The next diagonal (tens place) includes the bottom part of the top-right cell (4) and the bottom part of the bottom-left cell (4). Wait, but the bottom part of the bottom-left cell is 4, and the top part of the bottom-right cell is 0. Hmm, maybe I need to think step by step.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I should look at each diagonal line. Let me think of the grid as:\n\nFirst row cells:\n\nFirst cell (top-left): split into top-left (2) and bottom-right (8)\n\nSecond cell (top-right): split into top-left (1) and bottom-right (4)\n\nSecond row cells:\n\nThird cell (bottom-left): split into top-left (0) and bottom-right (4)\n\nFourth cell (bottom-right): split into top-left (0) and bottom-right (2)\n\nNow, the diagonals to sum are as follows, starting from the rightmost:\n\n1. The very rightmost diagonal: the bottom-right part of the bottom-right cell: 2. So units place is 2.\n\n2. Next diagonal to the left: the bottom-right of top-right cell (4) plus the bottom-right of bottom-left cell (4). Wait, no, actually, the diagonals cross through the cells. Let me see:\n\nThe first diagonal (units place): only the bottom-right of the bottom-right cell (2).\n\nSecond diagonal (tens place): the bottom-right of top-right cell (4) and the top-left of bottom-right cell (0). Wait, perhaps it's better to stack them:\n\nThe diagonals are read from bottom to top, right to left.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's easier to consider that after splitting each cell with a diagonal line from top-right to bottom-left, the numbers along each diagonal (from bottom-right to top-left) are summed.\n\nSo starting from the bottom-right corner:\n\nFirst diagonal (units place):\n\nOnly the bottom-right part of the bottom-right cell: 2.\n\nSecond diagonal (tens place):\n\nThe bottom-right part of the top-right cell (4) and the top-left part of the bottom-right cell (0). Wait, no, maybe it's the bottom-right of top-right (4) and the bottom part of bottom-left?\n\nHmm, this is getting confusing. Maybe this method isn't the best here. Let's go back to the standard algorithm which gave a clear 2982, so I can trust that.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can do the multiplication in another way, like 71\u00d742.\n\n71\u00d740 = 2840\n\n71\u00d72 = 142\n\nAdding them: 2840 +142 = 2982. Yep.\n\nAlright, so all methods are pointing to 2982. Therefore, I can confidently say the product of 42 and 71 is 2982.\n\nBut just to ensure I didn't make any arithmetic errors:\n\nLet me compute 42 \u00d771 step by step:\n\nFirst, 40\u00d770 = 2800.\n\nThen, 40\u00d71 =40.\n\nThen, 2\u00d770=140.\n\nThen, 2\u00d71=2.\n\nAdding all those together: 2800 +40 =2840; 2840+140=2980; 2980+2=2982. Perfect.\n\nAlternatively, 42\u00d770: 42\u00d77=294, so \u00d710 is 2940. Then add 42 gives 2982.\n\nYes, that's consistent.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 2982.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 42 and 71 is \\boxed{2982}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 42 and 71, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Write down the numbers vertically:\n   ```\n    42\n  \u00d7 71\n  ------\n   ```\n\n2. Multiply 42 by the units digit of 71 (which is 1):\n   \\[\n   42 \\times 1 = 42\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 42 by the tens digit of 71 (which is 7, representing 70). This result is shifted one position to the left:\n   \\[\n   42 \\times 70 = 42 \\times 7 \\times 10 = 294 \\times 10 = 2940\n   \\]\n\n4. Add the two results together:\n   \\[\n   42 + 2940 = 2982\n   \\]\n\nVerification using the distributive property:\n\\[\n42 \\times 71 = 42 \\times (70 + 1) = (42 \\times 70) + (42 \\times 1) = 2940 + 42 = 2982\n\\]\n\nAnother verification by breaking down the numbers:\n\\[\n42 \\times 71 = (40 + 2) \\times (70 + 1) = 40 \\times 70 + 40 \\times 1 + 2 \\times 70 + 2 \\times 1 = 2800 + 40 + 140 + 2 = 2982\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 42 and 71 is \\boxed{2982}.",
