nd 45 into 40 and 5, it works out to 3060.\n\nTherefore, the product of 68 and 45 is 3060.\n\nAlternatively, maybe another way to verify is using the standard multiplication:\n\n   68\n\n\u00d7 45\n\n--------\n\nMultiply 68 by 5: 5\u00d78=40, write down 0 and carryover 4. 5\u00d76=30 plus 4 is 34. So 340.\n\nThen multiply 68 by 40: since it's the tens place, we can do 68\u00d74=272, then add a zero to get 2720.\n\nAdding 340 and 2720: 340+2720. 340+2000=2340; 2340+720=3060. Yep, same result.\n\nTherefore, confident that the answer is 3060.\n\nWait, but in the initial problem, the user said \"Product of 68 and 45\". So my final answer should be 3060. But let me just confirm once more with another method, maybe the lattice method or something else?\n\nAlternatively, using the fact that 68\u00d745 = (70 - 2)\u00d745 = 70\u00d745 - 2\u00d745.\n\n70\u00d745: 7\u00d745=315, so 70\u00d745=3150.\n\n2\u00d745=90.\n\nTherefore, 3150 - 90 = 3060. Yep, same answer.\n\nAlright, so all methods lead to 3060. Therefore, the product is indeed 3060. Probably in the first approach, I mixed up the numbers and confused myself, but after correcting that, everything checks out.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 68 and 45 can be calculated using the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n\\[\n68 \\times 45 = (60 + 8) \\times (40 + 5)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n= 60 \\times 40 + 60 \\times 5 + 8 \\times 40 + 8 \\times 5\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n- \\(60 \\times 40 = 2400\\)\n- \\(60 \\times 5 = 300\\)\n- \\(8 \\times 40 = 320\\)\n- \\(8 \\times 5 = 40\\)\n\nAdding all the results together:\n\\[\n2400 + 300 + 320 + 40 = 3060\n\\]\n\n**Verification using standard multiplication:**\n```\n    68\n\u00d7  45\n-------\n   340  (68 \u00d7 5)\n+2720   (68 \u00d7 40, shifted one position to the left)\n-------\n  3060\n```\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product is **3060**.\n\n**Answer:** The product of 68 and 45 is 