600 = 1100, 58 + 20 = 78, so total 1178. Okay, so that matches the first method. So the answer should be 1178. But let me confirm with another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, 19 is 20 - 1. So 62 \u00d7 20 = 1240, then subtract 62, so 1240 - 62 = 1178. Yep, same result. Okay, so that's consistent. Therefore, I think the correct product is 1178.\n\nWait a second, but I initially messed up the numbers, confusing 52 and 37, but then corrected myself. Let me just verify once more with another approach. Let's do 62 \u00d7 19.\n\nBreaking down 19 as 10 + 9. So:\n\n62 \u00d7 10 = 620\n\n62 \u00d7 9 = 558\n\nAdding those together: 620 + 558. 600 + 500 is 1100, 20 + 58 is 78, so total 1178. Yep, same answer again. Alright, I'm confident now that the product of 62 and 19 is 1178. The key was making sure I didn't mix up the numbers from my initial mistake and verifying through multiple methods.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 62 and 19 can be calculated using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n62 \\times 19 = 62 \\times (20 - 1) = (62 \\times 20) - (62 \\times 1) = 1240 - 62 = 1178\n\\]\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n1. Break down 19 into \\(20 - 1\\).\n2. Multiply \\(62 \\times 20 = 1240\\).\n3. Multiply \\(62 \\times 1 = 62\\).\n4. Subtract the results: \\(1240 - 62 = 1178\\).\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe product of 62 and 19 is \\(\\boxed{1178}\\).",
