ust confirm once more.\n\nAlternatively, using another method: 88\u00d760 is 5280, plus 88\u00d71 is 88. Adding those gives 5280 +88=5368. Yep, that's consistent.\n\nTherefore, the product of 88 and 61 is 5368. I think that's correct. To be thorough, maybe do it another way.\n\nAlternatively, 61 is 60+1, so 88\u00d761 = 88\u00d7(60+1) = 88\u00d760 +88\u00d71. As above.\n\nAlternatively, 88\u00d761 can also be thought of as (90 - 2) \u00d761 = 90\u00d761 -2\u00d761.\n\nCompute 90\u00d761: 9\u00d761=549, so 90\u00d761=5490.\n\nThen subtract 2\u00d761=122. 5490 - 122 = 5368. Yep, same result.\n\nTherefore, I can confidently say the answer is 5368.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 88 and 61 can be calculated using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n88 \\times 61 = 88 \\times (60 + 1) = (80 + 8) \\times 60 + (80 + 8) \\times 1\n\\]\n\nBreaking it down:\n1. \\( 80 \\times 60 = 4800 \\)\n2. \\( 8 \\times 60 = 480 \\)\n3. \\( 80 \\times 1 = 80 \\)\n4. \\( 8 \\times 1 = 8 \\)\n\nAdding all parts:\n\\[\n4800 + 480 + 80 + 8 = 5368\n\\]\n\n**Verification** using standard multiplication:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 88 \\\\\n\\times 61 \\\\\n\\hline\n   \\quad 88 \\quad (\\text{88 \u00d7 1}) \\\\\n+ 5280 \\quad (\\text{88 \u00d7 60, shifted left by 1 digit}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   5368 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n**Final Answer:** \\(\\boxed{5368}\\)",
