thod again for verification.\n\n86 * 76 can be broken down as (80 + 6)*(70 + 6).\n\nWait, actually, 76 is 70 + 6. Wait no, 76 is 70 + 6, but 86 is 80 + 6. Let me compute each term:\n\n80*70 = 5600\n\n80*6 = 480\n\n6*70 = 420\n\n6*6 = 36\n\nAdding them all together: 5600 + 480 = 6080; 6080 + 420 = 6500; 6500 + 36 = 6536. So that matches the standard algorithm result. Therefore, the correct product is 6536.\n\nBut wait, in my initial mistake when I thought it was 51 and 13, I confused the numbers, but now I corrected it. So the answer should be 6536.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way to verify. Let me try the difference of squares or some other method?\n\nAlternatively, 86 * 76 can be written as (80 + 6)(80 - 4) since 76 is 80 - 4. Hmm, but not sure if that helps, but let's see:\n\n(80 + 6)(80 - 4) = 80^2 + 80*(-4) + 6*80 + 6*(-4) = 6400 - 320 + 480 -24 = 6400 -320 is 6080; 6080 +480 is 6560; 6560 -24 is 6536. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, using another method: 86 * 70 = 6020; 86 *6 = 516; adding them gives 6020+516=6536.\n\nYes, that's consistent. So I think that's the correct answer. Therefore, the product of 86 and 76 is 6536.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 86 and 76 is \\boxed{6536}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 86 and 76, we can use the standard algorithm and the distributive property for verification.\n\n**Standard Algorithm:**\n\n1. Multiply 86 by 6 (the ones place of 76):\n   \\[\n   86 \\times 6 = 516\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 86 by 70 (the tens place of 76, shifted one position to the left):\n   \\[\n   86 \\times 70 = 6020\n   \\]\n3. Add the partial products:\n   \\[\n   516 + 6020 = 6536\n   \\]\n\n**Distributive Property:**\n\n1. Break down 86 and 76 into their components:\n   \\[\n   (80 + 6) \\times (70 + 6)\n   \\]\n2. Calculate each term:\n   \\[\n   80 \\times 70 = 5600\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   80 \\times 6 = 480\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 70 = 420\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 6 = 36\n   \\]\n3. Sum all the terms:\n   \\[\n   5600 + 480 + 420 + 36 = 6536\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 86 and 76 is \\(\\boxed{6536}\\).",
