rly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe another method to check: 75\u00d754 can be written as (70 +5)(50 +4). Let's multiply that out:\n\n70\u00d750 = 3500,\n\n70\u00d74 = 280,\n\n5\u00d750 = 250,\n\n5\u00d74 =20.\n\nAdding all those together: 3500 +280 =3780, plus 250 is 4030, plus 20 is 4050. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, using the commutative property, 54\u00d775 is same as 75\u00d754. Maybe 54\u00d775: 54\u00d770 = 3780 and 54\u00d75=270, so total is 3780+270=4050.\n\nAll methods agree. So the correct product is 4050. Therefore, the final answer is 4050.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 75 and 54 is \\boxed{4050}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 75 and 54, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n\\[\n75 \\times 54 = 75 \\times (50 + 4)\n\\]\n\nApplying the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n75 \\times 50 + 75 \\times 4\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part separately:\n\n\\[\n75 \\times 50 = 3750\n\\]\n\\[\n75 \\times 4 = 300\n\\]\n\nAdding the results together:\n\n\\[\n3750 + 300 = 4050\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using another method, such as the standard algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   75 \\\\\n\\times 54 \\\\\n\\hline\n   300 \\quad (\\text{75} \\times 4) \\\\\n+ 3750 \\quad (\\text{75} \\times 50) \\\\\n\\hline\n   4050 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 75 and 54 is \\(\\boxed{4050}\\).",
