 of 53 as 50 +3, so 27\u00d750 is 1350, and 27\u00d73 is 81. Adding them gives 1350 +81=1431. Yep, same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the standard algorithm again but written out differently:\n\n27  \n\u00d753  \n------  \nFirst, 7\u00d73=21, write down 1, carry 2.  \nThen 2\u00d73=6 plus 2 is 8. So first partial product is 81.\n\nThen, moving to the tens digit (5), we start with a zero placeholder in the ones place.  \n5\u00d77=35, write down 5, carry 3.  \n5\u00d72=10 plus 3 is 13. So the second partial product is 1350.\n\nAdding 81 and 1350 gives 1431. So that's consistent.\n\nHmm, so I think that's correct. But let me check with a calculator approach. Since I can't use a calculator, but perhaps approximate to check.\n\n27\u00d750 is 1350, and 27\u00d73 is 81, so total 1350+81=1431. So that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, 53\u00d727: 53\u00d730 would be 1590, but subtract 53\u00d73 (which is 159) gives 1590 - 159 = 1431. That also works. Yep, same answer.\n\nTherefore, the product is 1431.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 27 and 53 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Break down the multiplication using the standard algorithm:**\n   - Multiply 27 by the ones digit of 53 (3):\n     \\[\n     27 \\times 3 = 81\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 27 by the tens digit of 53 (5), and shift the result one place to the left (since 5 represents 50):\n     \\[\n     27 \\times 5 = 135 \\quad \\rightarrow \\quad 135 \\times 10 = 1350\n     \\]\n\n2. **Add the partial products:**\n   \\[\n   81 + 1350 = 1431\n   \\]\n\n3. **Verification using distributive property:**\n   \\[\n   27 \\times 53 = (20 + 7)(50 + 3) = (20 \\times 50) + (20 \\times 3) + (7 \\times 50) + (7 \\times 3)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 1000 + 60 + 350 + 21 = 1431\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:** The product of 27 and 53 is \\boxed{1431}.",
