s! That also gives the same answer. So that's another method.\n\nTherefore, all methods point to 3589. Wait, but just to be 100% sure, maybe I can compute it step by step again.\n\nLet me do 97 times 37:\n\nStarting with the units place: 7 * 7 is 49, write down 9 and carryover 4.\n\nThen 7*9 is 63 plus the carried over 4 is 67. Write down 7 and carryover 6.\n\nNext, moving to the tens place (the 3 in 37, which represents 30):\n\nStart with 0*3 (since we're in the tens place now, so it's actually 30*97). Wait, actually, when doing the multiplication step-by-step:\n\nWait, perhaps it's clearer:\n\n          97\n        x37\n        -----\n          679  (this is 97 x7)\n         291   (this is 97x3, shifted one position to the left because it's the tens place)\n        -----\n         3589\n\nWait, actually, the second line should be 97*30, so 2910, not 291. So when written out:\n\n          97\n        x37\n        -----\n          679  (97x7)\n        +2910  (97x30)\n        -----\n         3589\n\nYes, that's correct. So adding 679 + 2910 gives 3589. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator? But since I can't, I have to rely on manual calculations. Since all methods give me 3589, I think that's the correct answer. Let me see if there's any chance I made a mistake in the subtraction method earlier.\n\nWait, in my first approach, I thought of 97 as 100 - 3, which is correct. So 100x37 is 3700, minus 3x37=111, so 3700-111. Let's compute that again: 3700 minus 100 is 3600, minus 11 more is 3589. Yes, correct. So that's the same as before.\n\nHmm, so all methods confirm that it's 3589. Therefore, I think that's the correct product of 97 and 37. I don't see any errors in the calculations now. Maybe I was confused initially when I started thinking of different numbers, but after correcting myself and using multiple methods, it's consistent.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 97 and 37 is \\boxed{3589}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 97 and 37, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Breaking down using the distributive property:**\n   - Compute \\(97 \\times 30\\):\n     \\[\n     97 \\times 30 = 97 \\times 3 \\times 10 = 291 \\times 10 = 2910\n     \\]\n   - Compute \\(97 \\times 7\\):\n     \\[\n     97 \\times 7 = (90 \\times 7) + (7 \\times 7) = 630 + 49 = 679\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     2910 + 679 = 3589\n     \\]\n\n2. **Using the complement method:**\n   - Express 97 as \\(100 - 3\\):\n     \\[\n     (100 - 3) \\times 37 = 100 \\times 37 - 3 \\times 37\n     \\]\n   - Compute each part:\n     \\[\n     100 \\times 37 = 3700 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 3 \\times 37 = 111\n     \\]\n   - Subtract the second result from the first:\n     \\[\n     3700 - 111 = 3589\n     \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 97 and 37 is \\(\\boxed{3589}\\).",
