t vertically:\n\n\u2003\u200388\n\u00d7\u00a066\n-------\n?\n\nStarting with the ones place of the bottom number (6), multiply by 88:\n\n88 \u00d7 6 = ?\n\n8\u00d76 = 48, write down 8 and carry over 4.\n\n8\u00d76 = 48 plus the 4 carried over is 52. So that gives 528.\n\nThen, moving to the tens place of the bottom number (6 again, but since it's in the tens place, it's actually 60), so we multiply 88 by 60.\n\n88 \u00d7 60:\n\nFirst, multiply 88 \u00d7 6 = 528 as before, then add a zero at the end because it's 60, so 5280.\n\nNow, add the two results together:\n\n528 (from 88\u00d76) + 5280 (from 88\u00d760) =\n\nAdding those:\n\n528 + 5280 = 5808? Wait, let me check:\n\n5280 + 528:\n\n5280 + 500 = 5780,\n\n5780 + 28 = 5808. Yes, that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, let me try using the distributive property again.\n\n88 \u00d7 66 can be written as (80 + 8) \u00d7 (60 + 6).\n\nExpanding this:\n\n80\u00d760 + 80\u00d76 + 8\u00d760 + 8\u00d76.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n80\u00d760 = 4800,\n\n80\u00d76 = 480,\n\n8\u00d760 = 480,\n\n8\u00d76 = 48.\n\nAdding them up: 4800 + 480 = 5280,\n\n5280 + 480 = 5760,\n\n5760 + 48 = 5808. Same result.\n\nAlternatively, another method: using the fact that 88 \u00d7 66 = (90 - 2) \u00d7 (60 + 6). Maybe that's more complicated, but let's see:\n\n(90 - 2)(60 + 6) = 90\u00d760 + 90\u00d76 - 2\u00d760 - 2\u00d76\n\n= 5400 + 540 - 120 -12\n\n= 5400 + 540 is 5940,\n\n5940 - 120 = 5820,\n\n5820 -12 = 5808. Yep, same answer.\n\nAlternatively, recognizing that 88 \u00d7 66 is the same as 88 \u00d7 (70 - 4) = 88\u00d770 - 88\u00d74.\n\n88\u00d770: 88\u00d77=616, so \u00d710 is 6160,\n\n88\u00d74 = 352,\n\n6160 - 352 = 5808.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using the formula (a+b)(a-b) but that might not apply here.\n\nAlternatively, using 88 \u00d7 66 = 8 \u00d7 11 \u00d7 6 \u00d7 11 = (8\u00d76) \u00d7 (11\u00d711) = 48 \u00d7 121. Wait, 11\u00d711 is 121, so 48\u00d7121.\n\nHmm, maybe that's more steps but let's see:\n\nFirst compute 48 \u00d7 121.\n\nBreaking down 121 into 100 + 20 +1,\n\n48\u00d7100 = 4800,\n\n48\u00d720 = 960,\n\n48\u00d71 =48.\n\nAdding them up: 4800 + 960 = 5760,\n\n5760 +48 =5808. Same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the identity (a \u00d7 b) = [(a + b)/2]^2 - [(a - b)/2]^2,\n\nBut that might be overcomplicating. Let me see:\n\nLet a =88, b=66,\n\n(a + b)/2 = (154)/2 =77,\n\n(a - b)/2 = (22)/2 =11,\n\nSo 77\u00b2 -11\u00b2 = 77\u00b2 -121,\n\n77 squared: 70\u00b2 +2\u00d770\u00d77 +7\u00b2 =4900 + 980 +49 = 5929,\n\n5929 -121 = 5808. Yep, that works too.\n\nSo all methods lead me to 5808. Therefore, the product of 88 and 66 is 5808.\n\nWait, but in my initial wrong problem (37\u00d743) I got 1591, but after correcting to 88\u00d766, all methods give 5808. So I need to make sure that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe a calculator check, but since I can't use a calculator, let me do another verification step.\n\nLet me compute 88 \u00d7 60 = 5280,\n\n88 \u00d7 6 = 528,\n\n5280 +528 =5808. Yep, that's straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, 66 \u00d7 88: since multiplication is commutative, same result.\n\nAlternatively, 66\u00d780=5280,\n\n66\u00d78=528,\n\nAdding those: 5280 +528= same as before.\n\nSo, I think 5808 is the correct answer.\n\nI must have started thinking about a different problem initially (37\u00d743) but caught myself and corrected it. So the final answer should be 5808.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 88 and 66 is \\boxed{5808}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 88 and 66, we can use the standard multiplication method:\n\n1. Write the numbers vertically:\n   ```\n    88\n  \u00d7 66\n  ------\n  ```\n\n2. Multiply 88 by 6 (the ones place of 66):\n   ```\n    88\n  \u00d7  6\n  -----\n    528\n  ```\n\n3. Multiply 88 by 60 (the tens place of 66, which is 6 shifted one position to the left):\n   ```\n    88\n  \u00d7 60\n  -----\n   5280\n  ```\n\n4. Add the two results together:\n   ```\n     528\n  + 5280\n  -------\n    5808\n  ```\n\nWe can also verify this using the distributive property:\n- Break down the numbers: \\(88 = 80 + 8\\) and \\(66 = 60 + 6\\).\n- Expand the product:\n  \\[\n  (80 + 8)(60 + 6) = 80 \\times 60 + 80 \\times 6 + 8 \\times 60 + 8 \\times 6\n  \\]\n- Calculate each term:\n  \\[\n  80 \\times 60 = 4800, \\quad 80 \\times 6 = 480, \\quad 8 \\times 60 = 480, \\quad 8 \\times 6 = 48\n  \\]\n- Sum the results:\n  \\[\n  4800 + 480 + 480 + 48 = 5808\n  \\]\n\nThus, the product of 88 and 66 is \\(\\boxed{5808}\\).",
