ror here. Let me check 48*60 again. 48*6=288, so times 10 is 2880. Correct. 48*5 is indeed 240. Adding them gives 3120. Alternatively, 48*65:\n\nBreaking down both numbers:\n\n48 = 40 + 8\n\n65 = 60 +5\n\nSo using the FOIL method:\n\n(40+8)(60+5) = 40*60 +40*5 +8*60 +8*5\n\nCalculate each term:\n\n40*60 = 2400\n\n40*5 = 200\n\n8*60 = 480\n\n8*5 =40\n\nAdding them all together: 2400 +200 =2600; 2600 +480 =3080; 3080+40=3120. Yep, that's the same.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use exponents? Not sure, but the multiplication checks out.\n\nI think 3120 is correct. Let me confirm with another method. Maybe using the fact that 48*65 = (48/2)*(65*2)=24*130=24*(100+30)=24*100 +24*30=2400 +720=3120. Yep, that works too.\n\nAlright, I think I've verified it multiple ways. So the product of 48 and 65 is 3120. Initially, I got confused with different numbers but after focusing, all methods lead to 3120. So that's the answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 48 and 65 is \\boxed{3120}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 48 and 65, we can use the distributive property. Breaking down the numbers:\n\n\\[\n48 \\times 65 = 48 \\times (60 + 5)\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(48 \\times 60\\):\n\n\\[\n48 \\times 60 = 48 \\times 6 \\times 10 = 288 \\times 10 = 2880\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(48 \\times 5\\):\n\n\\[\n48 \\times 5 = 240\n\\]\n\nAdd the results of the two calculations:\n\n\\[\n2880 + 240 = 3120\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using another method by breaking down both numbers:\n\n\\[\n(40 + 8)(60 + 5) = 40 \\times 60 + 40 \\times 5 + 8 \\times 60 + 8 \\times 5\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n\\[\n40 \\times 60 = 2400\n\\]\n\\[\n40 \\times 5 = 200\n\\]\n\\[\n8 \\times 60 = 480\n\\]\n\\[\n8 \\times 5 = 40\n\\]\n\nAdding these terms together:\n\n\\[\n2400 + 200 + 480 + 40 = 3120\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 48 and 65 is \\(\\boxed{3120}\\).",
