s.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using exponents? 45 is 9*5, 60 is 12*5, but not sure if that helps. Maybe 45*60 = (9*5)*(12*5) = 9*12*5*5 = 108*25. Then 108*25. 100*25 is 2500, 8*25 is 200, so total 2700. Yep, same result.\n\nSo it seems like all methods give 2700, so that must be the correct product. I think initially I confused the numbers and started with 30 and 28, which led to some confusion, but once I corrected that, everything lines up. Therefore, the product of 45 and 60 is 2700.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 45 and 60 is \\boxed{2700}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 45 and 60, we can use the distributive property and standard multiplication methods.\n\nFirst, we can break down the multiplication using the distributive property:\n\\[\n45 \\times 60 = 45 \\times (6 \\times 10) = (45 \\times 6) \\times 10\n\\]\nCalculating \\(45 \\times 6\\):\n\\[\n45 \\times 6 = (40 + 5) \\times 6 = 40 \\times 6 + 5 \\times 6 = 240 + 30 = 270\n\\]\nThen multiply by 10:\n\\[\n270 \\times 10 = 2700\n\\]\n\nAlternatively, using the standard algorithm:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   45 \\\\\n\\times 60 \\\\\n\\hline\n   0 \\quad \\text{(45 multiplied by 0)} \\\\\n2700 \\quad \\text{(45 multiplied by 60 shifted one position to the left)} \\\\\n\\hline\n2700 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 45 and 60 is \\(\\boxed{2700}\\).",
