ens digit of 3*2 (0), the units digit of 3*9 (7), and the units digit of 0*9 (0). So 0+7+0=7.\n\nThird diagonal (leftmost): the tens digit of 3*9 (2) and the tens digit of 0*9 (0). So 2 +0=2.\n\nSo combining those diagonals from left to right: 2, 7, 6. So the product is 2760. Which matches the previous result. So that's good. \n\nSo my initial mistake was probably splitting the numbers into the wrong parts. Maybe I broke 92 into 38 and 54 or something, but that complicates things. It's better to split into 90 and 2 for easier multiplication. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use the standard algorithm:\n\n   30\nx 92\n-------\n   60  (30 x 2)\n+2700  (30 x 90, but shifted one position to the left)\n-------\n  2760\n\nYep, that works. So the product is definitely 2760. \n\nI think the confusion earlier was due to a miscalculation when I was trying to use the distributive property but messed up the breakdown. Always better to stick with methods you know work, like the standard algorithm or lattice, if you're unsure. \n\nAnother way to check is using estimation. 30 times 90 is 2700, and 30 times 2 is 60, so adding them gives 2760. That's reasonable. If I had gotten something like 2415, that's way off, so estimation helps catch big errors. \n\nAlternatively, using calculator verification if allowed, but since this is a problem-solving exercise, assuming we have to do it manually. \n\nSo, after all that, I think the correct answer is 2760. The key was to ensure that the multiplication steps are done correctly without miscalculations, especially when breaking down numbers. It's easy to mix up digits or place values when doing mental math, so taking it step by step and verifying with different methods helps ensure accuracy.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 62 and 24 is calculated as follows:\n\n**Step-by-Step Solution:**\n\n1. **Break down the numbers using the distributive property:**\n   \\[\n   62 \\times 24 = 62 \\times (20 + 4)\n   \\]\n   \n2. **Multiply 62 by each part separately