\nAlternatively, using the standard algorithm:\n\n   17\nx 48\n-----\nFirst multiply 17 by 8: 17*8 = 136.\n\nThen multiply 17 by 40 (since the 4 is in the tens place). 17*40 = 680.\n\nAdd 136 + 680: 136 + 680. 136 + 600 = 736, plus 80 is 816.\n\nSo the result is 816.\n\nWait, let me verify with another method. Maybe using the distributive property differently.\n\nAlternatively, 48*17: 48*(10 + 7) = 480 + 336 = 816. Yep, that works too.\n\nAlternatively, using the lattice method or another approach, but I think that's sufficient. The answer should be 816.\n\nBut let me double-check with a calculator approach mentally. 17*48. Let's see, 15*48 = 720, plus 2*48 = 96, so 720 +96 = 816. Yep, same result. Okay, that seems consistent.\n\nSo the product of 17 and 48 is 816.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 17 and 48 is \\boxed{816}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 17 and 48, we can use the distributive property and the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nFirst, breaking it down using the distributive property:\n\\[\n17 \\times 48 = 17 \\times (40 + 8) = (17 \\times 40) + (17 \\times 8)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part:\n\\[\n17 \\times 40 = 680\n\\]\n\\[\n17 \\times 8 = 136\n\\]\n\nAdding the results together:\n\\[\n680 + 136 = 816\n\\]\n\nVerification using the standard algorithm:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   17 \\\\\n\\times 48 \\\\\n\\hline\n   136 \\quad (\\text{17 multiplied by 8}) \\\\\n+ 680 \\quad (\\text{17 multiplied by 40, shifted one position to the left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   816 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAnother verification using mental calculation:\n\\[\n15 \\times 48 = 720 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2 \\times 48 = 96\n\\]\n\\[\n720 + 96 = 816\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 17 and 48 is \\(\\boxed{816}\\).",
