of 16 and 88. Hmm...\"\n\nBut the user's question was 72 and 37. So maybe that was just a random mistake in my thinking, but I corrected it. So as long as my final answer is correct, that's okay. But I need to ensure that in the final answer, I provide 2664.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I should check once more with another method. Let me try the lattice method for multiplication.\n\nWait, maybe that's overcomplicating, but let's try.\n\nFor lattice multiplication of 72 \u00d737:\n\nSet up a 2x2 grid since both numbers are two digits.\n\nThe digits of 72 go on top: 7 and 2.\n\nThe digits of 37 go on the right: 3 and 7.\n\nDivide each cell into diagonals.\n\nFirst cell (top-left): 7\u00d73=21. Write 2 on the top diagonal and 1 on the bottom.\n\nSecond cell (top-right): 2\u00d73=6. So write 0 and 6? Wait, no, maybe I'm mixing up.\n\nWait, actually, lattice multiplication requires multiplying each digit pair.\n\nWait, maybe I should do it step by step:\n\nThe lattice grid for 72 \u00d737:\n\nColumns for 7 and 2 (top), rows for 3 and 7 (right).\n\nSo four cells:\n\nTop row (7 and 2) over columns, and right column (3 and 7) as rows.\n\nSo the four cells are:\n\nFirst cell (7\u00d73), second cell (2\u00d73), third cell (7\u00d77), fourth cell (2\u00d77).\n\nWait, actually, rows and columns might be arranged differently, but let me think:\n\nThe first digit of the first number (7) is multiplied by the first digit of the second number (3). Then 7\u00d77, then 2\u00d73, then 2\u00d77.\n\nWait, perhaps arranging the grid as:\n\nThe top number's digits are on the top, and the right number's digits are on the right. Each cell is divided by a diagonal.\n\nSo:\n\nFirst cell (top-left): 7\u00d73=21. Write 2 above the diagonal and 1 below.\n\nSecond cell (top-right): 2\u00d73=6. Write 0 above and 6 below? Wait, no, maybe it's 2\u00d73=6, so 0 and 6?\n\nWait, maybe I should look up the steps, but since I can't, let me try to recall.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to skip lattice