ns place): the lower part of the top-right cell (4) and the lower part of the bottom-left cell (0). Wait, no, actually each cell contributes to a diagonal.\n\nAlternatively, here's a better approach. The lattice method's diagonals are constructed such that each diagonal corresponds to a digit place. Starting from the lower right corner:\n\nFirst diagonal (units): the lower right cell's lower half, which is 0.\n\nSecond diagonal (tens): the lower half of the top-right cell (4) and the lower half of the bottom-left cell (0). Wait, no. Actually, each cell's lower half is part of the lower diagonal. Maybe I need to sum the numbers along the diagonals.\n\nAlternatively, here's a step-by-step way:\n\nAfter multiplying and placing the digits, you add along the diagonals from the bottom right to top left.\n\nThe first diagonal (units place): the bottom-right cell's lower half: 0.\n\nSecond diagonal (tens place): the top half of the bottom-right cell (1) plus the bottom half of the top-right cell (4). Wait, no, perhaps:\n\nActually, each diagonal is formed by the numbers along a diagonal line from top-right to bottom-left.\n\nLooking at the grid:\n\nThe top-left cell has 1 above the diagonal and 6 below.\n\nThe top-right cell has 0 above and 4 below.\n\nThe bottom-left has 4 above and 0 below.\n\nThe bottom-right has 1 above and 0 below.\n\nSo the diagonals for addition are:\n\nFirst diagonal (units): the \"4\" from the top-right cell's lower part and the \"0\" from the bottom-right lower? Wait, no.\n\nAlternatively, the rightmost diagonal is just the 4 from the top-right's lower and the 0 from the bottom-right's lower? Hmm, perhaps I need to look up the lattice method steps again mentally.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I'm overcomplicating. Since standard multiplication gives 2050 and two other methods also confirm that, maybe I can just stick with that.\n\nAlternatively, let's do 25*80=2000, then 25*2=50, totaling 2050. That's straightforward.\n\nWait, but in my initial problem,