      "text": "324.\n\nWait, hold on, that doesn't seem right. Let me try again. Maybe I should do the multiplication properly. 27 times 72. Let's break it down. 27 times 70 is 1890, and 27 times 2 is 54. Adding those together gives 1890 plus 54, which is 1944. But wait, why did I get two different answers? I must have messed up the first method. Let me try another way. Maybe using the standard algorithm.\n\nWriting it out:\n\n\u2003\u200327\n\u00d7 72\n-------\nFirst multiply 27 by 2: that's 54.\nThen multiply 27 by 70 (since the 7 is in the tens place): 27\u00d770 = 1890.\nAdd them together: 54 + 1890 = 1944.\n\nOkay, so that's consistent. But why did my first method give me 324? Oh, I must have confused the numbers. The first sentence mentioned 69 times 88, which is totally different. Maybe I was daydreaming. Anyway, sticking to the standard method, 27\u00d772 is definitely 1944. Let me verify with another approach, like the distributive property.\n\n27\u00d772 can be written as (20 + 7)(70 + 2). Expanding that:\n\n20\u00d770 = 1400,\n\n20\u00d72 = 40,\n\n7\u00d770 = 490,\n\n7\u00d72 = 14.\n\nAdding them all together: 1400 + 40 = 1440; 1440 + 490 = 1930; 1930 +14 = 1944. Yep, same answer. So I think that's correct. Maybe the first time I confused the numbers with a different problem. So the product of 27 and 72 is 1944. I should stick with that.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 27 and 72 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together. \n\nFirst, break down the multiplication using the distributive property:\n\\[\n27 \\times 72 = 27 \\times (70 + 2) = (27 \\times 70) + (27 \\times 2)\n\\]\n\nCalculate each part:\n- \\(27 \\times 70 = 1890\\)\n- \\(27 \\times 2 = 54\\)\n\nAdd the results:\n\\[\n1890 + 54 = 1944\n\\]\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe product of 27 and 72 is \\boxed{1944}.",
