 so I think that's correct. Maybe I was overcomplicating earlier, but now I'm confident. Therefore, the answer should be 869.\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me do a quick check with approximate estimation. 11 times 80 is 880, so subtracting 11 gives 880 -11= 869. Exactly. That's a good check. So yes, that's correct. Okay, so the product is 869.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 11 and 79 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Breakdown using distributive property**:\n   \\[\n   11 \\times 79 = 11 \\times (70 + 9)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = (11 \\times 70) + (11 \\times 9)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 770 + 99 = 869\n   \\]\n\n2. **Verification using standard multiplication**:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n      \\quad 11 \\\\\n    \\times 79 \\\\\n    \\hline\n      \\quad 99 \\quad (\\text{11 \u00d7 9}) \\\\\n    + 770 \\quad (\\text{11 \u00d7 70, shifted left by 1}) \\\\\n    \\hline\n      869 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Estimation check**:\n   \\[\n   11 \\times 80 = 880 \\quad \\text{(since 80 is 1 more than 79)}\n   \\]\n   Subtract 11 (since \\(79 = 80 - 1\\)):\n   \\[\n   880 - 11 = 869\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer**: \\(\\boxed{869}\\)",
