      "text": "4238. Wait, that doesn't seem right. Maybe I messed up somewhere.\n\nLet me start over. Multiplying 81 by 55. Maybe using the standard algorithm. Write down 81, multiply by 55. So first multiply 81 by 5, which is 405. Then multiply 81 by 50, which is 4050. Then add them together: 405 + 4050 gives 4455. Hmm, but earlier I got 4238. Which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the first method. Let me check again. If I do 57 times 02, that's 114, and then adding 69 gives 114 + 69 = 183. So 183? That can't be right because 57 times 2.3 is way higher. Maybe my breakdown was wrong.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should just stick to the standard multiplication. Let's do 69 times 31. Let me write it down:\n\n   69\nx 31\n-------\nFirst multiply 69 by 1, which is 69. Then multiply 69 by 30, which is 2070. Add them together: 69 + 2070 = 2139. Wait, that's different from both previous answers. Now I'm confused.\n\nWait, the question was actually 90 and 31? Let me check the original question again. The user wrote: \"What is the Product of 90 and 31?\" Oh no! I misread the numbers. I was solving 69 times 31 or 81 times 55 instead of 90 times 31. That's why I was getting confused.\n\nAlright, let's start fresh with the correct numbers. The problem is 90 multiplied by 31. Okay, so 90 times 30 would be 2700, and then 90 times 1 is 90, so adding those gives 2700 + 90 = 2790.\n\nAlternatively, using the standard algorithm:\n\n   90\nx 31\n-------\nMultiply 90 by 1: 90\nMultiply 90 by 30: 2700\nAdd them: 90 + 2700 = 2790.\n\nThat seems straightforward. Let me confirm with another method. Breaking down 31 into 30 + 1. So 90*(30 +1) = 90*30 + 90*1 = 2700 + 90 = 2790. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, 31 times 90 is the same as 31*9*10. 31*9 is 279, then times 10 is 2790. That works too.\n\nHmm, so all methods give me 2790. I think that's the correct answer. I just had to make sure I was using the right numbers after my initial mistake. Phew! Glad I caught that.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 90 and 31 is \\boxed{2790}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 90 and 31, we can use the standard multiplication method:\n\n1. Multiply 90 by 1 (the units place of 31):\n   \\[\n   90 \\times 1 = 90\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 90 by 30 (the tens place of 31):\n   \\[\n   90 \\times 30 = 2700\n   \\]\n3. Add the results of the two multiplications:\n   \\[\n   90 + 2700 = 2790\n   \\]\n\nAnother method is to break down 31 into 30 + 1:\n\\[\n90 \\times 31 = 90 \\times (30 + 1) = 90 \\times 30 + 90 \\times 1 = 2700 + 90 = 2790\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify by recognizing that:\n\\[\n31 \\times 90 = 31 \\times 9 \\times 10 = 279 \\times 10 = 2790\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 90 and 31 is \\(\\boxed{2790}\\).",
