 + 2) = 13*90 + 13*2. \n\n13*90: Well, 10*90=900 and 3*90=270, so 900+270=1170. \n\n13*2=26. \n\nAdding them together: 1170 +26 = 1196. That matches my previous result. \n\nAlternatively, I can do 13*92 as 13*(100 - 8) = 13*100 - 13*8 = 1300 - 104 = 1196. \n\nHmm, same answer. So that must be correct. \n\nWait, but let me confirm with another method. Maybe using the distributive property differently. \n\nAlternatively, 13*92 = (10 + 3)*(90 + 2) = 10*90 + 10*2 + 3*90 + 3*2 \n\nCalculating each term: \n\n10*90 = 900\n\n10*2 = 20\n\n3*90 = 270\n\n3*2 = 6\n\nAdding them all together: 900 +20 = 920; 920 +270 = 1190; 1190 +6 = 1196. \n\nSame result again. Okay, so that seems consistent. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using a calculator? Wait, but since I should do it manually. Let me check multiplication another way. \n\n13 multiplied by 92. Let's reverse the numbers: 92*13. \n\nSame thing:\n\n\u2003\u00a092\n\u00d7 13\n-------\n?\n\n3*92 = 276\n\n10*92 = 920\n\nAdding 276 + 920 = 1196. \n\nSame result. \n\nTherefore, I believe the correct answer is 1196. \n\nWait, but hold on, in my initial thinking process I was confused with different numbers, but after correcting myself, all methods point to 1196. So I think that's the right answer. \n\nBut just to be thorough, perhaps I can do the multiplication step by step again:\n\nMultiplying 13 by 92:\n\nFirst, write 13\n\n\u00d792\n\nStarting with the 2 (units place):\n\n2 \u00d7 3 = 6\n\n2 \u00d7 1 = 2. So first partial product is 26.\n\nThen the 9 (tens place, so it's actually 90):\n\n9 \u00d7 3 = 27, write down 7 and carryover 2.\n\n9 \u00d71 = 9, plus the carried over 2 is 11. So the second partial product is 117, but shifted one position to the left, making it 1170.\n\nAdding 26 + 1170:\n\n26 + 1170. 26 + 1170: 1170 + 20 = 1190, plus 6 is 1196. \n\nYes, that's consistent. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using lattice multiplication? Let me try that for fun. \n\nSet up a 2x2 lattice since both numbers are two digits. \n\nThe digits of 13 are 1 and 3; the digits of 92 are 9 and 2. \n\nSo the lattice grid:\n\n1 | 3\n\n9 | 2\n\nWait, actually, lattice multiplication is a bit different. Let me recall. You write the numbers on the top and right side. \n\nSo for 13 \u00d7 92, the top would be 1 and 3, and the right side would be 9 and 2. Then you multiply each digit pair and add diagonally. \n\nBut maybe this is complicating. Let me see. \n\nFirst cell (top 1, right 9): 1\u00d79=9, so write 9 in the top triangle and 0 in the bottom for the tens place.\n\nSecond cell (top 1, right 2): 1\u00d72=2, so 2 in the top, 0 in the bottom.\n\nThird cell (top 3, right 9): 3\u00d79=27, so 2 in the top, 7 in the bottom.\n\nFourth cell (top 3, right 2): 3\u00d72=6, so 6 in top, 0 in bottom.\n\nThen you add along the diagonals. The diagonals from right to left:\n\nFirst diagonal (the very rightmost): the bottom of the last cell is 0.\n\nSecond diagonal: the bottom of the third cell (7) plus the top of the fourth cell (6) = 13. So write down 3 and carryover 1.\n\nThird diagonal: the bottom of the second cell (0) + top of third cell (2) + bottom of first cell (0) + carried over 1 = 3. \n\nFourth diagonal: the top of the first cell (9). \n\nWait, maybe I messed up the lattice setup. Hmm, perhaps it's better not to overcomplicate here. Since all other methods give 1196, I can be confident that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, just add 13 ninety-two times, but that would be tedious. \n\nAlternatively, think of 13*90 = 1170 and 13*2=26, so total 1170+26=1196. Yep. \n\nSo after all these checks, the product of 13 and 92 is 1196. I think that's solid. The initial confusion was because I started with the wrong numbers, but after correcting, everything lines up. So I'm confident the answer is 1196.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 13 and 92 is \\boxed{1196}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 13 and 92, we can use the standard multiplication method:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 13 \\\\\n\\times 92 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nFirst, multiply 13 by the units digit of 92 (which is 2):\n\n\\[\n13 \\times 2 = 26\n\\]\n\nNext, multiply 13 by the tens digit of 92 (which is 9, representing 90), and shift the result one position to the left:\n\n\\[\n13 \\times 9 = 117 \\quad \\text{(which becomes 1170 when shifted left)}\n\\]\n\nNow, add the two partial products together:\n\n\\[\n26 + 1170 = 1196\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using different methods:\n- Breaking down 92 into 90 and 2:\n  \\[\n  13 \\times 90 = 1170 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 13 \\times 2 = 26\n  \\]\n  Adding these results:\n  \\[\n  1170 + 26 = 1196\n  \\]\n\n- Using the distributive property:\n  \\[\n  13 \\times 92 = 13 \\times (90 + 2) = 13 \\times 90 + 13 \\times 2 = 1170 + 26 = 1196\n  \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 13 and 92 is \\(\\boxed{1196}\\).",
