 let me verify once more to be sure. 11 multiplied by 60. Since 60 has a zero at the end, multiplying by 60 is the same as multiplying by 6 and then by 10. So 11*6 = 66, then times 10 is 660. Yep, that checks out. \n\nAlternatively, using another method: 11 times 60 can be thought of as 10*60 + 1*60 = 600 + 60 = 660. That also works. \n\nSo regardless of the method, the result is 660. Therefore, the product of 11 and 60 is 660.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 11 and 60 is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n11 \\times 60 = 11 \\times (6 \\times 10) = (11 \\times 6) \\times 10 = 66 \\times 10 = 660\n\\]\n\n**Answer:** 660",
