onfusion earlier was because I misread the numbers initially, but upon correcting, all methods agree. So I can confidently say the answer is 1209.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 31 and 39 can be calculated using the distributive property for easier computation:\n\n\\[\n31 \\times 39 = 31 \\times (40 - 1) = (31 \\times 40) - (31 \\times 1)\n\\]\n\nBreaking it down:\n- \\( 31 \\times 40 = 1240 \\)\n- \\( 31 \\times 1 = 31 \\)\n\nSubtracting these results:\n\\[\n1240 - 31 = 1209\n\\]\n\n**Verification with vertical multiplication:**\n```\n   31\n\u00d7 39\n-------\n  279  (31 \u00d7 9)\n+930   (31 \u00d7 30, shifted one position left)\n-------\n 1209\n```\n\nBoth methods confirm the result is \\(\\boxed{1209}\\).",
