tent.\n\nAlternatively, using another approach, maybe adding 67 eighty-nine times? Well, that's impractical, but perhaps breaking it down differently.\n\nAlternatively, 89 is 100 - 11, so:\n\n67*(100 -11) = 67*100 -67*11 = 6700 - 737 = 6700 -700=6000, then subtract 37 more: 6000-37=5963. Same result again.\n\nHmm, so all methods give me 5963. So I think that's the correct answer. The initial mistake I had was when I was talking about 02 and 23, but that must have been a typo or brain fart, since the actual question is 67 and 89. So the correct answer is 5963.\n\nBut wait, just to ensure, let me check with a calculator method in my mind. 67 times 90 is 6030, subtract 67 is 5963. Alternatively, 70 times 89 is 6230, subtract 3 times 89 (267) gives 6230-267=5963. Yep.\n\nAlright, so confident now the answer is 5963.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 67 and 89 is \\boxed{5963}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 67 and 89, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller parts. Here are the key steps:\n\n1. **Express 89 as 90 - 1**:\n   \\[\n   67 \\times 89 = 67 \\times (90 - 1)\n   \\]\n2. **Calculate \\(67 \\times 90\\)**:\n   \\[\n   67 \\times 90 = 67 \\times 9 \\times 10\n   \\]\n   - First, compute \\(67 \\times 9\\):\n     \\[\n     60 \\times 9 = 540 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 7 \\times 9 = 63\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     540 + 63 = 603\n     \\]\n   - Then multiply by 10:\n     \\[\n     603 \\times 10 = 6030\n     \\]\n\n3. **Subtract \\(67 \\times 1\\)**:\n   \\[\n   6030 - 67 = 5963\n   \\]\n\n4. **Verification using the standard algorithm**:\n   - Multiply 67 by 9:\n     \\[\n     67 \\times 9 = 603\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 67 by 80 (since 8 is in the tens place):\n     \\[\n     67 \\times 80 = 5360\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     603 + 5360 = 5963\n     \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 67 and 89 is \\(\\boxed{5963}\\).",
