      "text": "99\u00d754= 574.\n\nWait, hold on, that can't be right. Let me try a different approach. Maybe breaking down the numbers using the distributive property?\n\nSo, 99 \u00d7 54 can be written as (100 - 1) \u00d7 54. Which is 100\u00d754 minus 1\u00d754. 100\u00d754 is 5400, minus 54 gives 5400 - 54 = 5346. Wait, so that's conflicting with my previous result. So which one is correct?\n\nHmm, so the first method I did with the standard algorithm I must have made a mistake. Let me redo that.\n\nLet me try again:\n\nMultiplying 99 by 54.\n\nFirst, write it as:\n\n\u2003 99\n\u00d7 54\n-------\n.\n\nStarting with the ones place of the bottom number (4):\n\n4 \u00d7 9 = 36. Write down 6, carry over 3.\n\n4 \u00d7 9 = 36, plus the carried 3 is 39. Write down 9 and carry over 3? Wait, no.\n\nWait, actually, let me do it step by step. First, multiply 4 (from 54) by each digit of 99 from right to left:\n\nFirst digit: 9 \u00d7 4 = 36. Write down 6, carry over 3.\n\nSecond digit: 9 \u00d7 4 = 36, plus the carried 3 is 39. So write down 9 and carry over 3? Wait, but there are no more digits, so actually that carryover should be added as another digit. Wait, no, perhaps the second digit is the tens place. Wait, maybe I'm confusing the steps here.\n\nWait, perhaps breaking it down:\n\nFirst partial product (4 \u00d7 99):\n\n4\u00d79 = 36. Write down 6, carryover 3.\n\n4\u00d79 (the next digit to the left, which is the tens place) = 36 + 3 carried over is 39. So that gives us 396.\n\nWait, so the first partial product is 396.\n\nThen, moving to the next digit in the bottom number, which is the 5 (tens place, so it's 50). So we need to multiply 99 by 50, which is the same as 99\u00d75\u00d710.\n\nFirst, 99\u00d75:\n\n9\u00d75=45, write down 5, carryover 4.\n\n9\u00d75=45 + 4 = 49. So that gives 495, then multiplied by 10 gives 4950.\n\nThen add 396 + 4950.\n\n396 + 4950: 396 + 4000 is 4396, plus 950 is 5346. So that matches the distributive method. So the correct answer