      "text": "1 plus 1 is 2, 5 plus 4 is 9, and 2 plus 7 is 9. So 992? Wait, that doesn't seem right. Maybe I made a mistake in breaking it down.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should use the standard algorithm. Let's try that. Multiply 67 by 14. Starting with the ones place: 7 times 4 is 28. Write down the 8 and carry over the 2. Then, 6 times 4 is 24, plus the carried over 2 is 26. So that gives me 268. Then, moving to the tens place, since it's the second digit, we add a zero first. Then 7 times 1 is 7, and 6 times 1 is 6. So that gives 67. Adding 268 and 670. Wait, 268 plus 670 is 938. Hmm, but earlier I got 992. Which is correct?\n\nWait, maybe I confused the numbers. The original problem was 17 times 55. Oh no, I thought it was 65 and 16. Let me check again. The user wrote: \"What is the product of 17 and 55?\" So it's 17 multiplied by 55.\n\nAlright, let's start over. 17 times 55. Maybe breaking it into parts would be better. I know that 55 is 50 plus 5. So, 17 times 50 plus 17 times 5. Let's compute each part.\n\nFirst, 17 times 50. Well, 17 times 5 is 85, so times 10 would be 850. Wait, no. Wait, 50 is 5 times 10, so 17*5=85, then 85*10=850. Wait, that seems correct.\n\nThen, 17 times 5. That's 85 again. So adding them together: 850 plus 85 is 935. Hmm, so maybe the answer is 935?\n\nAlternatively, let me try the standard algorithm. 17 multiplied by 55.\n\nWrite it down:\n\n   17\nx 55\n------\nFirst multiply 17 by 5 (the ones place of 55):\n\n17 * 5 = 85. Write down 85.\n\nThen multiply 17 by 50 (the tens place, so shift one position to the left):\n\n17 * 50 = 850. So write that down as 850.\n\nAdd the two results: 85 + 850 = 935. So that's 935.\n\nWait, but in my first mistaken calculation, I had different numbers because I misread the question. So the correct answer should be 935.\n\nAlternatively, another way: 17 times 55. Since 55 is half of 110, maybe that's not helpful. Alternatively, 17*55 = (20 - 3)*55 = 20*55 - 3*55. Let's compute that.\n\n20*55 is 1100. 3*55 is 165. So 1100 - 165 = 935. Yep, same answer.\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is 935. Let me confirm with another method. Maybe using the distributive property differently. 17*55 = 17*(50+5) = 17*50 +17*5 = 850 +85 = 935. Yep, same as before. I think that's solid. So the product of 17 and 55 is 935.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 17 and 55 is \\boxed{935}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 17 and 55, we can use the distributive property by breaking down 55 into 50 and 5:\n\n\\[\n17 \\times 55 = 17 \\times (50 + 5)\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(17 \\times 50\\):\n\n\\[\n17 \\times 50 = 17 \\times 5 \\times 10 = 85 \\times 10 = 850\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(17 \\times 5\\):\n\n\\[\n17 \\times 5 = 85\n\\]\n\nAdd the two results together:\n\n\\[\n850 + 85 = 935\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify this using the standard algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   17 \\\\\n\\times 55 \\\\\n\\hline\n   85 \\quad (\\text{17} \\times 5) \\\\\n+850 \\quad (\\text{17} \\times 50) \\\\\n\\hline\n  935 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAnother method using the distributive property with \\(20 - 3\\):\n\n\\[\n17 \\times 55 = (20 - 3) \\times 55 = 20 \\times 55 - 3 \\times 55\n\\]\n\nCalculate each term:\n\n\\[\n20 \\times 55 = 1100 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 3 \\times 55 = 165\n\\]\n\nSubtract the results:\n\n\\[\n1100 - 165 = 935\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 17 and 55 is \\(\\boxed{935}\\).",
