 Let me think. If I can express them as numbers around a common base?\n\nAlternatively, maybe (50 +1)(30 +3). Let's expand that:\n\n50*30 +50*3 +1*30 +1*3 = 1500 +150 +30 +3 = 1500+150 is 1650, plus 30 is 1680, plus 3 is 1683. Same result again.\n\nAlright, so all the methods are giving me 1683. So probably that's the correct answer. But let me just verify using a calculator approach.\n\nAlternatively, 51 multiplied by 33. Let's do 51 * 33. Let me do 51 * 30 =1530, and 51*3=153, so adding them gives 1530 +153. Let me add 1530 + 153. 1530 + 100 is 1630, then +53 is 1683. Yep.\n\nAlternatively, if I do 33 * 50 =1650, plus 33*1=33, so 1650 +33=1683. Same.\n\nHmm, so all methods lead me to 1683. Therefore, the product of 51 and 33 is 1683. I think that's correct. Let me just ensure there's no calculation mistake. 51 times 33. Let me try multiplying them directly:\n\n   51\nx33\n------\n  153  (this is 51*3)\n+1530 (this is 51*30, shifted one position to the left)\n------\n 1683\n\nYes, that adds up. So the answer should be 1683. I think that's solid. I don't see any errors in the steps. So I'm confident that's the right product.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 51 and 33 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Breakdown using distributive property**:\n   - \\( 51 \\times 33 = 51 \\times (30 + 3) \\)\n   - \\( = (51 \\times 30) + (51 \\times 3) \\)\n\n2. **Calculate each part**:\n   - \\( 51 \\times 30 = 1530 \\)  \n     *(because \\( 50 \\times 30 = 1500 \\) and \\( 1 \\times 30 = 30 \\), so \\( 1500 + 30 = 1530 \\))*\n   - \\( 51 \\times 3 = 153 \\)  \n     *(because \\( 50 \\times 3 = 150 \\) and \\( 1 \\times 3 = 3 \\), so \\( 150 + 3 = 153 \\))*\n\n3. **Sum the results**:\n   - \\( 1530 + 153 = 1683 \\)\n\n**Final Answer**:  \n\\boxed{1683}",
