ing with another method, I arrived at the same answer. So I think that's the right one.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 67 and 58 is \\boxed{3886}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 67 and 58, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n(60 + 7)(50 + 8)\n\\]\n\nBreaking this down into smaller parts:\n\n1. Calculate \\(60 \\times 50\\):\n   \\[\n   60 \\times 50 = 3000\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(60 \\times 8\\):\n   \\[\n   60 \\times 8 = 480\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate \\(7 \\times 50\\):\n   \\[\n   7 \\times 50 = 350\n   \\]\n\n4. Calculate \\(7 \\times 8\\):\n   \\[\n   7 \\times 8 = 56\n   \\]\n\nAdding all these results together:\n\n\\[\n3000 + 480 + 350 + 56\n\\]\n\nFirst, add \\(3000 + 480\\):\n\\[\n3000 + 480 = 3480\n\\]\n\nNext, add \\(3480 + 350\\):\n\\[\n3480 + 350 = 3830\n\\]\n\nFinally, add \\(3830 + 56\\):\n\\[\n3830 + 56 = 3886\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify this by another method. Using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   67 \\\\\n\\times 58 \\\\\n\\hline\n   536 \\quad (\\text{67} \\times 8) \\\\\n+3350 \\quad (\\text{67} \\times 50) \\\\\n\\hline\n  3886 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 67 and 58 is \\(\\boxed{3886}\\).",
