itial thinking, I was confused with different numbers, maybe I was looking at another problem. Let me make sure I didn't mix up numbers.\n\nAnother way: 67 \u00d7 31. Let me think of it as (60 +7)*(30 +1). Using the FOIL method (First, Outer, Inner, Last):\n\nFirst: 60\u00d730 = 1800.\n\nOuter: 60\u00d71 = 60.\n\nInner: 7\u00d730 = 210.\n\nLast: 7\u00d71 = 7.\n\nAdding all those together: 1800 + 60 is 1860. 1860 + 210 is 2070. 2070 +7 is 2077. Yep, same result. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using some other multiplication trick. For example, since 31 is close to 30, which is easier. But I think the methods I've tried all point to 2077. \n\nWait, but let me do it step by step again just to be thorough. \n\nMultiplying 67 by 31:\n\nStart with the units digit of the lower number (1):\n\n67 \u00d7 1 = 67. \n\nThen, the tens digit (3) but shifted one position to the left (because it's the tens place):\n\n67 \u00d7 3 = 201, but since it's the tens place, it's 2010. \n\nAdding 67 and 2010 gives 2077. \n\nAlternatively, using calculator verification if I can. Wait, since I can't use a calculator, but 67\u00d730 is 2010, plus 67 is indeed 2077. \n\nTherefore, the product of 67 and 31 is 2077. \n\nWait, but just to be extra sure, let me try another approach. Let me compute 67\u00d731 as 67\u00d7(30 +1). \n\nSo 67\u00d730: 67\u00d710=670, so times 3 is 2010. Then add 67, so 2010 +60 is 2070, plus 7 is 2077. Yep, same. \n\nAlternatively, 31\u00d767. Maybe reversing the numbers. Let's see 31\u00d760 is 1860, plus 31\u00d77 is 217. So 1860 +217 is 2077. \n\nYes, that works too. \n\nSo I think the answer is 2077. I don't see any errors in my calculations now. Initially, I got confused because I was thinking of different numbers, but focusing on 67 and 31, all methods give the same result. Therefore, I'm confident that the product is 2077.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 67 and 31 is \\boxed{2077}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 67 and 31, we can use the distributive property. Breaking down 31 into 30 + 1, we have:\n\n\\[\n67 \\times 31 = 67 \\times (30 + 1) = 67 \\times 30 + 67 \\times 1\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\( 67 \\times 30 \\):\n\n\\[\n67 \\times 30 = 67 \\times 3 \\times 10 = 201 \\times 10 = 2010\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\( 67 \\times 1 \\):\n\n\\[\n67 \\times 1 = 67\n\\]\n\nAdding these two results together:\n\n\\[\n2010 + 67 = 2077\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 67 \\\\\n\\times 31 \\\\\n\\hline\n   \\quad 67 \\quad (\\text{67 \u00d7 1}) \\\\\n+ 2010 \\quad (\\text{67 \u00d7 30}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   2077 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 67 and 31 is \\(\\boxed{2077}\\).",
