tep:\n\n60\n\nx31\n\nFirst, multiply 60 by 1: 60\n\nThen multiply 60 by 30 (since the 3 is in the tens place, it's 3*10). So 60*30. \n\nCalculating 60*30: \n\n6*3 =18, and there are two zeros (one from 60 and one from 30), so total 1800. \n\nAdding 60 + 1800: 1860. Perfect.\n\nAlternatively, breaking down 31 into 30 +1 was the right approach. \n\nAlternatively, thinking of 60*31 as (6*10)*(3*10 +1). So 6*3=18, 10*10=100, but maybe that complicates.\n\nAlternatively, using the area model: a rectangle with sides 60 and 31. Divide it into 60 and 1, so 60*30 and 60*1. Total area is sum. \n\nAll methods lead to 1860, so I feel confident that's the correct answer. \n\nI think my initial confusion was because I misread the numbers as 55 and 80, which led me down a wrong path, but catching that mistake and starting over with the correct numbers helped me arrive at the right solution. It's a good reminder to always double-check the problem statement before proceeding. \n\nAnother point to note is that when using the distributive property, breaking numbers into tens and ones (or other convenient chunks) can simplify the multiplication. In this case, splitting 31 into 30 and 1 made the calculation straightforward. \n\nIn summary, the key steps are:\n\n1. Read the problem correctly: 60 and 31.\n2. Choose a multiplication strategy (distributive property here).\n3. Break down the numbers appropriately.\n4. Perform the multiplications separately.\n5. Add the results.\n6. Verify using an alternative method to ensure accuracy.\n\nThis systematic approach helps prevent errors and builds confidence in the solution.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 60 and 31 is \\boxed{1860}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 60 and 31, we can use the distributive property by breaking down 31 into 30 and 1:\n\n\\[\n60 \\times 31 = 60 \\times (30 + 1)\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate each part separately:\n- \\( 60 \\times 30 = 1800 \\)\n- \\( 60 \\times 1 = 60 \\)\n\nThen, add the results together:\n\\[\n1800 + 60 = 1860\n\\]\n\nVerification using the standard multiplication method:\n- Multiply 60 by 1: \\( 60 \\times 1 = 60 \\)\n- Multiply 60 by 30: \\( 60 \\times 30 = 1800 \\)\n- Add the results: \\( 60 + 1800 = 1860 \\)\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 60 and 31 is \\(\\boxed{1860}\\).",
