21, so 240+21=261.\n\nAdding 3480 + 261: 3480 + 200 is 3680, plus 61 is 3741. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the difference of squares or some other trick? Let's see if that's possible.\n\nHmm, 87 and 43. Maybe express them as numbers around multiples of 10 or something. Let's see, 87 is 90 - 3, and 43 is 40 + 3. Not sure if that helps.\n\nAlternatively, ( (80 +7) \u00d7 43 ). But that's similar to the previous method.\n\nAlternatively, 87\u00d743 can be thought of as (90 - 3) \u00d743 = 90\u00d743 - 3\u00d743 = 3870 - 129 = 3870 - 100 is 3770, minus 29 is 3741. That works too. So that also gives the same answer.\n\nOkay, so all methods point to 3741. So the initial mistake was probably due to a calculation error when I first did the standard algorithm. So the correct product is 3741. \n\nWait a moment, let me check once more with calculator steps. 87 multiplied by 43.\n\nCompute 87*40 = 3480, 87*3=261. 3480+261 = 3741. Yep. So that's correct.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 3741.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 87 and 43 is \\boxed{3741}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 87 and 43, we can use the distributive property and the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nFirst, using the distributive property:\n\\[\n(80 + 7) \\times (40 + 3)\n\\]\nExpanding this, we get:\n\\[\n80 \\times 40 + 80 \\times 3 + 7 \\times 40 + 7 \\times 3\n\\]\nCalculating each term:\n\\[\n80 \\times 40 = 3200\n\\]\n\\[\n80 \\times 3 = 240\n\\]\n\\[\n7 \\times 40 = 280\n\\]\n\\[\n7 \\times 3 = 21\n\\]\nAdding these results together:\n\\[\n3200 + 240 + 280 + 21 = 3200 + 240 = 3440\n\\]\n\\[\n3440 + 280 = 3720\n\\]\n\\[\n3720 + 21 = 3741\n\\]\n\nUsing the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   87 \\\\\n\\times 43 \\\\\n\\hline\n  261 \\quad (\\text{87} \\times 3) \\\\\n+3480 \\quad (\\text{87} \\times 40) \\\\\n\\hline\n  3741 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 87 and 43 is \\(\\boxed{3741}\\).",
