 3*2 add 156. So that checks out.\n\nHmm, so I think the answer is 2236. Let me just go through the vertical multiplication again to be sure.\n\n   43\nx 52\n-------\nFirst step: multiply 43 by 2 (units digit):\n\n3*2 = 6, write down 6.\n\n4*2 = 8, write down 8. So first partial product is 86.\n\nSecond step: multiply 43 by 5 (tens digit). But since it's the tens place, we shift one to the left.\n\n3*5 = 15, write down 5 and carry over 1.\n\n4*5 = 20, plus the carried 1 is 21. So write 21 next to the 5, so that's 215, but shifted left, so it's 2150.\n\nAdding 86 + 2150:\n\n2150 + 80 is 2230, plus 6 is 2236. Yep, that's the same as before.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is 2236. I can't see any mistakes in my calculations here. All methods cross-check to the same result. So I'm confident that's the right product of 43 and 52.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 43 and 52 is \\boxed{2236}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 43 and 52, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm and verify using the distributive property.\n\nFirst, using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Multiply 43 by 2 (units place of 52):\n   \\[\n   43 \\times 2 = 86\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 43 by 5 (tens place of 52), and shift one position to the left:\n   \\[\n   43 \\times 5 = 215 \\quad \\text{(shifted left to become 2150)}\n   \\]\n3. Add the partial products:\n   \\[\n   86 + 2150 = 2236\n   \\]\n\nUsing the distributive property:\n\\[\n43 \\times 52 = (40 + 3) \\times (50 + 2)\n\\]\nExpanding the terms:\n\\[\n40 \\times 50 + 40 \\times 2 + 3 \\times 50 + 3 \\times 2\n\\]\nCalculating each term:\n\\[\n40 \\times 50 = 2000\n\\]\n\\[\n40 \\times 2 = 80\n\\]\n\\[\n3 \\times 50 = 150\n\\]\n\\[\n3 \\times 2 = 6\n\\]\nAdding these together:\n\\[\n2000 + 80 + 150 + 6 = 2236\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 43 and 52 is \\(\\boxed{2236}\\).",
