duct: 93\u00d79 = 837,\n\nSecond partial product: 93\u00d770 = 6510,\n\nTotal sum: 837+6510=7347.\n\nYes, so that's correct. Therefore, the answer should be 7347.\n\nBut in my first calculation when I wrote the example with 85\u00d724, I might have confused myself with different numbers, but when I focused on the actual problem, the steps are correct. Therefore, I think the correct product is 7347.\n\nWait, just to confirm once more with another method:\n\n93 \u00d779.\n\nAlternatively, 93\u00d7(80 -1) = 93\u00d780 -93\u00d71 = 7440 -93 = 7347.\n\nYes, that works. So I'm confident the answer is 7347.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 93 and 79 is \\boxed{7347}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 93 and 79, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Multiply 93 by 9 (the ones digit of 79):\n   \\[\n   93 \\times 9 = 837\n   \\]\n   - \\(9 \\times 3 = 27\\), write down 7 and carry over 2.\n   - \\(9 \\times 9 = 81\\), plus the carried-over 2 gives 83.\n\n2. Multiply 93 by 70 (the tens digit of 79, which is 7 in the tens place, so 70):\n   \\[\n   93 \\times 70 = 6510\n   \\]\n   - Multiply 93 by 7:\n     - \\(7 \\times 3 = 21\\), write down 1 and carry over 2.\n     - \\(7 \\times 9 = 63\\), plus the carried-over 2 gives 65.\n   - Shift the result one position to the left: 6510.\n\n3. Add the two partial products together:\n   \\[\n   837 + 6510 = 7347\n   \\]\n\nVerification using the distributive property:\n\\[\n(90 + 3)(70 + 9) = 90 \\times 70 + 90 \\times 9 + 3 \\times 70 + 3 \\times 9\n\\]\n\\[\n= 6300 + 810 + 210 + 27 = 7347\n\\]\n\nAnother verification using the difference of squares:\n\\[\n93 \\times 79 = 93 \\times (80 - 1) = 93 \\times 80 - 93 \\times 1 = 7440 - 93 = 7347\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 93 and 79 is \\(\\boxed{7347}\\).",
