h no! I think I got confused. Let me check again.\n\nThe user's question is: \"What is the Product of 79 and 66?\" But in my thinking process, I started with 61 and 71, then switched to 35 and 52. That's a mistake. I must have been daydreaming or something. Let me correct this.\n\nSo the actual question is 79 multiplied by 66. Let me do that properly now.\n\nAlright, so 79 x 66. Let me try both methods again but correctly this time.\n\nFirst, standard algorithm:\n\nWrite down:\n\n   79\n\nx 66\n\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 79 by 6 (the ones place of 66):\n\n79 x6. Let's compute that. 70*6=420, 9*6=54. So 420 +54=474. So write down 474.\n\nThen multiply 79 by 60 (since the next digit is the tens place, so 6 in tens place is 60). So 79 x60. To compute that, 79 x6=474, then add a zero at the end, making it 4740.\n\nNow add the two results: 474 + 4740. Let's see, 474 + 4740. 4740 + 400 is 5140, plus 74 is 5214. So total is 5214?\n\nWait, but let me check with another method to confirm. Let's use distributive property.\n\n79 x66 can be written as (70 +9)(60 +6). Expanding that:\n\n70*60 +70*6 +9*60 +9*6\n\nCompute each term:\n\n70*60 = 4200\n\n70*6 = 420\n\n9*60 = 540\n\n9*6 = 54\n\nNow add all those together: 4200 + 420 is 4620, plus 540 is 5160, plus 54 is 5214. So that matches the standard algorithm. So the product of 79 and 66 is 5214.\n\nWait a second, but let me confirm once more with another method to be absolutely sure. Maybe the difference of squares or some other trick?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps using rounding. Let me see:\n\n79 x66. Maybe think of 79 as 80 -1, and 66 as is. So (80 -1)*66 = 80*66 -1*66.\n\n80*66: 80*60=4800, 80*6=480, so total 4800+480=5280. Then subtract 66: 5280 -66 = 5214. Same result.\n\nAlternatively, 66 can be considered as 70 -4. Wait, but perhaps that complicates. Alternatively, 79*66= (75+4)*(60+6). Hmm, maybe overcomplicating, but let's try:\n\n75*60 = 4500, 75*6=450, 4*60=240, 4*6=24. So 4500+450=4950, +240=5190, +24=5214. Yep, same